Version 5.0 (unreleased)
========================

1. Constraint Diff is removed. Its zero-length rectangles followed neither
   the strict nor the non-strict semantics of diffn. Use
   new Diffn(rectangles, true) (strict, minizinc diffn) or
   new Diff2(rectangles) (non-strict, minizinc diffn_nonstrict) instead;
   Diff.builder() becomes Diffn.builder()...strict(true).build() or
   Diff2.diff2Builder(). With positive lengths all of them accept the
   same solutions, but they may search differently. Diff2, Disjoint and
   DisjointConditional, which extended Diff, now extend the package-private
   AbstractDiff and keep their behaviour. The Scala diff2 wrappers now post
   Diff2, as documented, instead of Diff.

2. The MiniZinc solver configuration org.jacop.msc declares -debug, so
   minizinc --solver org.jacop -debug model.mzn prints the constraints
   as imposed in JaCoP; before, minizinc rejected -debug and it had to
   be passed with --fzn-flags -debug. The MiniZinc IDE shows it as a
   solver option.

3. New constraint CircuitCost(x, costs, cost): x is a circuit and cost is
   the sum of its arc costs. It bounds the cost and removes arcs with the
   assignment relaxation (incremental Hungarian method, reduced costs) and,
   for symmetric costs, the Held-Karp 1-tree bound. The FlatZinc front end
   posts it as a redundant constraint on circuit(x) with
   cost = sum(i)(d[i, x[i]]); -Dorg.jacop.fz.circuitCost=false turns this off.

4. CumulativeUnary (and CumulativeUnaryOptional, MiniZinc disjunctive) is
   about 2x faster on job shop models with the same pruning. The overload
   check and detectable precedences run in linear time after sorting on
   the union-find time line of Fahimi, Ouellet and Quimper (Constraints,
   2018) instead of the Theta tree; not-last reads ect(Theta \ {i}) from
   the tree without changing it; edge finding builds its tree bottom up;
   and the bounds of the tasks are read once per pass and again only after
   a rule changed them. Search trees are unchanged.

5. CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive) runs its unary rules (detectable
   precedences, not-first/not-last, edge finding) for any number of tasks.
   Before, with more than 100 tasks (max_edge_find_size) it ran only the
   time-table profile, contrary to properties.txt; a single machine with
   400 tasks then timed out where it is now solved in 808 nodes, and job
   shops with 150 and 200 jobs reach better makespans. max_edge_find_size
   now applies to Cumulative only.

6. New constraint IncreasingSum(x, s, strict): x is increasing (strictly
   or not) and s is its sum, bounds consistent in linear time per call
   (Petit, Regin and Beldiceanu, CP 2011). The FlatZinc front end posts it
   as a redundant constraint on an int_lin_eq or int_lin_le with unit
   coefficients whose variables are ordered by increasing/decreasing or by
   chains of x[i] <= x[i+1] from symmetry breaking;
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.increasingSum=false turns this off. On number
   partitioning it cuts failures up to 13x.

7. Constraints have a degree weight, Constraint.setDegreeWeight(w): the
   number of constraints each counts as in the static degree of its
   variables, Var.sizeConstraintsOriginal(), which MostConstrainedStatic
   reads (FlatZinc most_constrained, occurrence, impact and
   smallest_most_constrained). It is 1 by default; Var.adjustDegree(n)
   credits a variable with constraints a reformulation removed. The
   redundant constraints that the FlatZinc front end adds (disjunctive
   cliques, CircuitCost, IncreasingSum) have weight 0, so they no longer
   change the variable order of these searches, and a store derived by
   presolve (org.jacop.presolve.Derivation) keeps the static degrees of
   its parent.

8. A FlatZinc model without search annotation is solved with free search,
   as with -f (dom/wdeg, restarts, nogoods), instead of labelling its
   variables in input order with domain splitting. On 78 such models
   (MiniZinc benchmarks and the regression set, 60 s) it answers 65
   instead of 54: queens n = 50 to 400 within 8 s instead of no solution,
   and jobshop la09, orb02 and orb10 proved. --default-search input
   restores the input order search. FreeSearch now reads the clock at
   every node, so a time limit holds when nodes are slow.

9. The set order constraints AleB and AltB (MiniZinc set_le and set_lt,
   sets compared as sorted lists of elements) are bounds consistent: an
   element stays undecided in a set only when the constraint has a
   solution with it and one without it, and the cardinalities are narrowed
   to those of the solutions. Before, they only removed the elements of b
   below min(lub(a)) and checked fixed sets. On steiner-triples from the
   MiniZinc benchmarks the search tree shrinks up to 4x.

10. The FlatZinc front end posts the jacop_count constraints (MiniZinc
    count with a fixed value) over the same list as one CountValues
    instead of one Count per value. As Count constraints each of them was
    queued on every change of a list variable and scanned the list, so
    magicseq with n = 300 found no solution in 60 s; it is now solved in
    0.2 s, and n = 500 in 0.7 s. CountValues also uses that the counts and
    the variables taking no counted value add up to the list length, so
    search trees can be smaller.

11. WeightedSpanningTree (MiniZinc weighted_spanning_tree, d_weighted_
    spanning_tree, dwst) has a cost bound for directed graphs too: the new
    ArborescenceBound computes Edmonds' minimum arborescence containing the
    selected edges, with a virtual root for an unknown root, and removes
    edges and root values whose reduced cost (Houndji, Schaus, Hounkonnou
    and Wolsey, CPAIOR 2017) exceeds the cost's upper bound. On degree-
    constrained minimum arborescences with 12 to 60 nodes (60 s) JaCoP
    proved 2 of 16 optima before and proves all 16 now, each within 0.7 s
    of search and 2,600 nodes; Gecode 6.4.0 proves 1 and OR-Tools CP-SAT
    9.15 proves 9.

12. New constraint IncreasingNValue(x, count): x is non-decreasing and
    takes count distinct values, domain consistent in time linear in the
    total size of the domains of x (Beldiceanu, Hermenier, Lorca and
    Petit, CPAIOR 2010). The FlatZinc front end posts it instead of Values
    for a jacop_nvalue (MiniZinc nvalue) whose variables are ordered, by
    the same order detection as IncreasingSum (now in IncreasingDetector);
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.increasingNValue=false turns this off. On generated
    slot-assignment models (symmetric tasks, minimise the slots used),
    four of fifteen instances that took 12 s, 40 s or more than 60 s are
    solved in under a second.

13. New balancing constraints Deviation(x, s, d) and Spread(x, s, d):
    sum(x) = s and d = sum(i)(abs(n * x[i] - s)), or the sum of
    (n * x[i] - s)^2, with general forms over abs(a * x[i] - b) and
    (a * x[i] - b)^2. They are bounds consistent on x for d >= cost and
    raise d.min to the least cost of an assignment with sum s (Schaus,
    Deville, Dupont and Regin, CP 2007; Schaus and Regin, EJCO 2014).
    The FlatZinc front end posts them as redundant constraints on a sum of
    int_abs or squared terms over variables whose sum an int_lin_eq fixes,
    as sum(p)(abs(n * load[p] - total)) with sum(load) = total compiles;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.balance=false turns this off. JaCoP's MiniZinc library
    adds the predicates deviation and spread. On 28 balanced academic
    curriculum instances with a deviation or spread objective JaCoP proves
    52 of the 54 optima in under 3 s each; without the new constraints it
    proves none in 20 s.

14. New constraint StockingCost(x, d, h, H, c) for lot sizing (Houndji,
    Schaus, Wolsey and Deville, CP 2014 and Constraints 2019): order i is
    produced in period x[i] <= d[i], at most c orders per period, and
    sum h[i] * (d[i] - x[i]) <= H. It bounds H by the greedy optimum of
    the relaxation without the lower bounds of x and raises the minima of
    x that the maximum of H allows (bounds consistent on the relaxation
    for equal costs). MiniZinc predicate stocking_cost in the JaCoP
    library; the FlatZinc front end also posts it as a redundant
    constraint on an alldifferent (or gcc) with a cost sum over the same
    variables; -Dorg.jacop.fz.stockingCost=false turns this off. On the
    lot sizing part of the 46 CSPLib prob058 instances it proves 39 optima
    in 60 s instead of 19.

15. New constraint UnaryTransition(s, d, family, transitions[, optional]):
    a unary resource with sequence-dependent transition (setup) times
    between families of tasks, also with optional tasks (Dejemeppe, Van
    Cauwelaert and Schaus, CP 2015; Van Cauwelaert, Dejemeppe and Schaus,
    J. Scheduling 2020). Overload, detectable precedences, not-last and
    edge finding run on Theta-Lambda trees whose completion times count a
    lower bound of the transitions among the families of their tasks, plus
    a pairwise rule. MiniZinc predicate disjunctive_setup in the JaCoP
    library (also over var opt int starts); the FlatZinc front end posts it
    instead of CumulativeUnary on a disjunctive clique whose disjunctions
    carry transition times. It explains its own propagation for lazy
    clause generation (--lcg): bounds, absent optional tasks and failures
    by pairs, cuts of tasks that must precede, not-first/not-last and
    edge-finding sets, found again from the bounds before the atom. On 38
    job shops and single machines with setups (60 s, --lcg) they raise
    the optima proved from 0-5 to 8-12, depending on the model and the
    search; before, the generic reason explained 83 to 86 % of the reasons.

16. The FlatZinc front end posts a redundant Knapsack on multi-dimensional
    knapsacks: a profit (a sum of 0/1 variables with positive, not all
    equal coefficients that is bounded from below, or that defines a
    variable) and capacity rows over the same variables (sums with
    positive coefficients bounded from above). The Knapsack is posted on
    one surrogate row, the capacity rows weighted by the dual values of
    their linear relaxation (a small bounded simplex), so it prunes with
    the bound of the relaxation of all rows together; each linear
    constraint alone sees only its own row. It is posted only when that
    bound fixes a variable at the root by its reduced cost.
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.knapsack=false turns this off. On the 7 multi-knapsack
    instances of the MiniZinc benchmarks JaCoP now finds the solution in
    under 0.3 s in every mode (default, -f, --lcg); before, it found none
    in 30 s on 5 of them.

17. New constraints InterDistance(x, p), |x[i] - x[j]| >= p for all pairs,
    bounds consistent in O(n^2) (Quimper, Lopez-Ortiz and Pesant, AAAI
    2006), and MultiInterDistance(x, m, p), at most m values in every
    window of p, bounds consistent (Ouellet and Quimper, IJCAI 2011). The
    mznlib has inter_distance and multi_inter_distance for them. The
    FlatZinc front end posts a cumulative with fixed equal durations p >= 2
    and fixed equal resource use as MultiInterDistance with m = limit div
    use (InterDistance for m = 1), and adds InterDistance to a disjunctive
    clique of equal fixed lengths; -Dorg.jacop.fz.interDistance=add keeps
    the cumulative too and =false turns this off. On 90 runway instances
    with time windows (one to three runways, 30 to 90 aircraft, 60 s) it
    answers 79 instead of 63; Gecode answers 59 and Chuffed 60.
18. New constraint Reservoir(times, changes, min, max) and a MiniZinc
    predicate reservoir in the JaCoP library: the level, the sum of the
    changes of the events at or before each time, stays within min..max.
    It propagates with the balance constraint of Laborie (AIJ 2003) over
    the precedences x + c <= y of the model, which the FlatZinc front end
    gives it. On 30 project scheduling instances with storages (60 s) it
    finds solutions on 29 instead of 25 and proves the optima both prove
    1.8 times faster than the decomposition.

19. New constraint EnergyPrecedence (Laborie, AIJ 2003): the tasks that
    must end before a time point (such as a makespan) need their energy
    between their earliest start and that point, and symmetrically for
    the tasks that must start after it. The FlatZinc front end posts it on
    every cumulative whose tasks are ordered by the precedences of the
    model; -Dorg.jacop.fz.energyPrecedence=false turns this off. On the
    560 cumulative benchmark instances (30 s) it proves 215 optima instead
    of 211 and loses none.

18. New constraint OverlappingAlldiff(s, t): alldifferent(s) and
    alldifferent(t) on lists that share variables, propagated together on
    the bounds (Bessiere, Katsirelos, Narodytska, Quimper and Walsh,
    AAAI 2010). It bounds how many shared and how many own variables of
    each list can take values in each interval, closes the bounds under
    additivity of the counts, and couples them through the two
    alldifferents, which detects every failure of the bound relaxation and
    applies the paper's rules 2a-2d. The FlatZinc front end posts it next
    to the two all_different constraints when they share at least half of
    the unfixed variables of each; -Dorg.jacop.fz.overlappingAlldiff=false
    turns this off. On the paper's random instances (three alldifferents
    sharing 10 to 12 variables, 90 instances, 60 s) JaCoP solves 80
    instead of 65; on the paper's pathological family it proves
    unsatisfiability at the root where it timed out from n = 4.

19. New set constraint AllDisjoint(s): the sets s are pairwise disjoint.
    Besides the pairwise rule of AdisjointB it uses the cardinalities of
    the sets (Bessiere, Hebrard, Hnich and Walsh, CP 2004): elements are
    assigned to sets, or to no set, by a flow that meets the minimal
    cardinalities, and the strongly connected components of its residual
    graph give bounds consistency on the set bounds. MiniZinc all_disjoint
    now posts it instead of n(n-1)/2 jacop_disjoint. Social golfers search
    the same trees as before; on project allocation models (sets of
    students with cardinality bounds, 8 to 32 projects, 60 s) it finds
    a first solution on 3 of 18 instances where the pairwise version finds
    none and a better objective on 8 more.

19. New constraint ChannelOrder(x, b, t, atMost): b[i] <=> x <= t[i] (or
    x > t[i]), the order counterpart of ChannelReif, with one propagator
    for all thresholds of x and explanations for lazy clause generation.
    Presolve (--presolve on|check) replaces the reified comparisons of one
    variable with constants (int_le_reif, int_lt_reif, int_ge_reif,
    int_gt_reif) by one ChannelOrder, a presolve rule;
    -Djacop.presolve.orderLiterals=false turns it off. On the 42 presolve
    dev-tier instances with such reifications (60 s) search is the same and
    solve time is 14 % lower (9 % with --lcg).

20. Presolve breaks variable symmetries (--presolve on|check, unless
    --symmetry off). The FlatZinc model becomes a coloured graph (Puget,
    CP 2005): variables coloured by domain, constraints by name and
    constants, argument positions by place, with the arguments whose order
    does not matter (linear terms, all_different, bool_clause, cumulative
    tasks, bin packing items, the two sides of int_eq, ...) given one
    colour. New org.jacop.presolve.GraphAutomorphisms finds generators of
    its automorphism group by individualization and refinement (as nauty,
    saucy and bliss), with the variables as the base in lex-leader order,
    within at most 1 s (-Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetryTime, in ms) and a
    twentieth of the time left. Each generator g posts X <=lex g(X).
    The objective variable is fixed by every symmetry, so optima are kept;
    solutions are lost, so it is off when a satisfaction problem asks for
    more than one solution (-a, -n). Pigeonhole with 10 pigeons and 9
    holes needs 2,392 failures instead of 764,943.

21. CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive) is faster again, with the same
    search trees: 4.6x on job shops (geometric mean over 23 instances from
    6x6 to 200x20; 1.2x to 40x, more tasks per machine gain more) and 2.2x
    on open shops. A fixed task that ends before every other task can
    start is left out of the unary rules and the time-table for the rest
    of the subtree; in a search that schedules from left to right these
    are most tasks. A rule that pruned nothing is not run again until a
    bound changes. With constant durations and resource uses a unary
    time-table replaces the sweep of the cumulative profile and removes
    the same values, holes included. Edge finding updates only the Lambda
    values on the path of a removed task and stops where they no longer
    change.

22. Steiner (MiniZinc steiner and dsteiner) has a cost bound: the new
    SteinerBound runs Wong's dual ascent on the directed cut relaxation
    (no LP solver) and, with the reduced costs, removes nodes and edges
    that no tree within max(cost) can use. Selected edges cost 0 and add
    their weight; a dsteiner root that is not fixed is replaced by a
    virtual root. It is posted only when no weight is negative. On
    geometric Steiner trees with 20 to 120 nodes and Steiner arborescences
    with 20 to 80 nodes (60 s) JaCoP proves all 28 optima in under 2 s,
    against 3 before.

23. Binpacking reasons on the number of items of each bin (bin packing
    with cardinality, Schaus et al. CP 2012; Derval, Regin and Schaus,
    Constraints 2018). A new optional argument card (constructor and
    builder) gives the number of items of each bin, items of weight 0
    included. The constraint bounds it from the loads (the fewest heaviest
    items that reach the minimum load, the most lightest items within the
    maximum) and from the total number of items, bounds the loads from it,
    and removes an item from a bin when the bin would then need too much
    or reach too little ("too big", "too small"). The FlatZinc front end
    passes the counts of a global_cardinality (or global_cardinality_low_up)
    over the same bin variables, as in curriculum models;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingCardinality=false turns it off. On 40
    generated balanced academic curriculum instances JaCoP proves 37 optima
    in 60 s against 30 before. Without card the rules are off by default;
    cardinalityReasoning(true) turns them on.

24. Subcircuit and Circuit explain their own propagation for lazy clause
    generation (--lcg), by cuts of the successor graph (Francis and
    Stuckey, Constraints 2014): an arc on no cycle, a node that cannot
    join the required ones, a node every path between two required nodes
    passes through (the dominator pruning), the fixed arcs of a chain for
    the arc that would close it early (Circuit), and Hall sets for the
    rest. Before, both used the generic reason: on mario (--lcg -f) every
    Subcircuit reason, 266 atoms on average; now none, with 9 atoms on
    average. On cvrp P-n16-k8 the Circuit reasons fall from 122 atoms
    (all generic) to 19. tpp is proved about 1.7x faster; mario, cvrp,
    is, p1f and stochastic-vrp end as before within 60 s.

25. --lcg -f finds solutions on cutstock and largescheduling, where it
    found none in 60 s. Under lazy clause generation the time table of
    Cumulative keeps to the bounds of the starts: a hole of thousands of
    start values was thousands of atoms, each with its own literals. Its
    explanations look for overloads only where the compulsory-part load
    changes, weigh energy windows in one sweep per window start, and explain
    a task left with no start instead of giving the generic reason. Without
    a search annotation, the runs of LCG free search alternate with dom/wdeg
    on the default search variables, as they alternate with an annotation.
    On the 66 MiniZinc benchmark instances with cumulative or without an
    annotation (60 s), 3 go from no solution to a solution, 3 more are
    proved optimal, and none lose a solution or a proof.

26. --lcg works on models with set variables; before, it printed "--lcg
    ignored: the model has float or set variables" and searched without
    learning. Each set variable is mirrored by a 0/1 variable for the
    membership of each element of its least upper bound that is not in its
    greatest lower bound and by a variable for its cardinality (new
    constraint org.jacop.set.constraints.SetMirror). Every change of the
    set's domain is passed to the mirror inside the constraint that made
    it, so the mirror's atoms have that constraint as reason and stand for
    the set in the generic explanations of the set constraints (new
    interface org.jacop.lcg.Mirrored). The explanation checker
    (-Djacop.lcg.check=true) checks the set constraints too. Under --lcg
    the decisions of a set_search annotation on an element are taken as
    the literal that the element is in the set. Set constraints that hold
    element by element (AintersectBeqC, AunionBeqC, AdiffBeqC, AinB, AeqB,
    AdisjointB, EinA) explain a membership by the memberships of the same
    element in their other sets, and SetMirror explains what the
    cardinality gives, instead of the generic reason over every element.
    AleB and AltB (set_le, set_lt) explain a membership by the memberships
    and cardinalities that leave their order automaton no solution with
    the other membership, found by relaxing them one at a time
    (steiner-triples 09: 1.1 s instead of 10.9 s). Float variables still
    turn --lcg off.

27. Lazy clause generation explains XmulYeqZ removals by bounds first: the
    product range of x and y (squares when x = y), the quotient range of a
    factor, and the square roots of z for x * x = z, citing only the
    bounds that matter. Before, a removal of more than 64 values, or with a
    factor ranging over 1024 values or more, got the generic reason. On gbac
    (squares over 0..1566) generic reasons drop from about half of the
    XmulYeqZ reasons to almost none and the reasons from 17 to 2 atoms;
    over the 46 MiniZinc benchmark instances with a variable product
    (--lcg -f, 60 s) the solved ones take 2 % less time (geometric mean)
    and gbac reaches a better objective on 5 of 6.

27. New constraint GlobalCardinality(x, values, counters) and
    GlobalCardinality(x, values, low, up): domain consistent global
    cardinality (Regin, AAAI 1996) on x, with the counters as intervals;
    values outside values are not restricted. The FlatZinc front end posts
    it for global_cardinality (jacop_count_values, _bounds and the closed
    forms) annotated :: domain, which used to be ignored. It explores the
    search tree of Gecode's domain consistent gcc. On rows and columns
    with cardinality bounds and forbidden values it solves 8 of 12 random
    instances in 60 s where counting solves 6, with 3x to 900x fewer
    nodes; on Challenge models that do not ask for :: domain counting is
    as good and cheaper, so it stays the default.

28. FlatZinc objective presolve (the objective equation shifted by the
    exactly-one sets of the model) runs without --presolve too, with
    degree weight 0. It reads n = count(x, v) (jacop_count) as
    n = sum(x[i] = v) when every x[i] has a literal for v, and the shifted
    cost terms of each variable encoded by value literals become one cost
    variable y = c[x] (ElementInteger). The reformulated equation replaces
    the constraint defining the objective when it has no more variables,
    and is used only when it gives cost variables or has at most half the
    variables of the definition; a stronger root bound alone did not pay
    on vrp under --lcg. On jp-encoding (60 s) JaCoP proves data300 in
    4.4 s instead of no proof and data200 in 17 s with --lcg -f, and
    data1400 under --lcg -f ends at 62,978 instead of 68,422;
    bus_scheduling r1a is proved in 3.1 s instead of not proved.
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.objectivePresolve=false turns it off.

28. With lazy clause generation (--lcg) an equality (XeqY, and int_eq_reif
    and int_eq_imp in FlatZinc) gives a variable of more than 10,000
    values only the bounds of the other one, not its holes: every value
    removed between the bounds was a != atom with its own literals, so
    copying a sparse domain into a large one made millions of them. On
    liner-sf-repositioning fm3_5, --lcg -f created 8.9 million literals in
    10 nodes and ran out of memory; it now finds 60 solutions in 60 s with
    119 thousand literals (tp7_10 too goes from an error to a solution).
    Of the 258 vs-ortools instances (60 s, --lcg -f) the limit changes 6;
    groupsplitter u15g3pref2 also improves (232 against 181-189) and the
    others keep their result. -Djacop.lcg.holeLimit changes the limit.

29. New constraint AlldiffSum(x, rel, s): alldifferent(x) and
    sum(x) rel s, rel ==, <= or >=, bounds consistent for <= and >=
    (Beldiceanu, Carlsson, Petit and Regin, ECAI 2012, O(n log n)). The
    FlatZinc front end posts it next to each int_lin_eq / int_lin_le with
    unit coefficients over variables that all_different constraints make
    pairwise different (killer sudoku cages, kakuro runs, magic square
    rows); -Dorg.jacop.fz.alldiffSum=false turns this off. Enumerating all
    solutions of 9x9 killer sudokus with cages of up to 5 or 7 cells needs
    58 % fewer nodes; with free search 22x22 kakuro are solved in 10 s
    instead of 115 s with one left unsolved. gardner_sum_square finds
    another optimal solution.

30. The FlatZinc front end also posts the redundant SubcircuitCost when a
    successor is fixed and written as a constant, as MiniZinc writes
    succ[LuigiHouse] = MarioHouse in mario; before, the detection gave up on
    such a subcircuit. All 6 mario instances of the OR-Tools comparison
    are now proved in 60 s: in 0.6 to 13 s under --lcg -f (2 before) and
    0.6 to 38 s by default (2 before).

31. Free search (-f, with and without --lcg) tries the cost variables of
    FlatZinc objective presolve first, cheapest value first (largest when
    maximising), before the annotation it alternates with, and with
    --lcg gives core-guided search 0.1 of the time instead of 0.5 when
    there are such variables. On jp-encoding (60 s, --lcg -f) the four
    large instances end 0.3% to 2.4% above the optimum instead of 13% to
    57% (data1400: 44,693 against 68,403, optimum 43,626), and data200
    and data300 are proved in about 7 s. Models without cost variables
    search as before.

32. The FlatZinc front end adds the linear relaxation of the model's linear
    constraints (org.jacop.constraints.lp.LinearRelaxation) as a
    propagator; --lp off turns it off. A bounded dual simplex, warm-started
    from the previous node, solves it during search. A Farkas certificate
    of an infeasible relaxation, or the duals of the objective, are
    rounded to integer multipliers and the combination of the rows is
    computed exactly, so each deduction is a valid linear inequality,
    explained by LinearForm under --lcg. Its cost is bounded: solves spend
    a work credit earned by each call (a solve that only tightens bounds
    a sixteenth of its work, a solve that fails the node refills it), a
    simplex stalled at its pivot limit 32 times in a row is given up, and models whose tableau would exceed 4M entries are left
    out (-Dorg.jacop.lp.maxTableau). On the 414 vs-ortools instances
    (20 s) JaCoP proves 116 instead of 106 (153 instead of 147 with
    --lcg) and loses no proof, including wwtpp-real UNSAT instances in
    about a second and rel2onto optima. LinearExplanation now merges a
    variable that occurs with weights of both signs and refuses reasons
    whose sums overflow a long.

33. --lcg -f branches on defined variables last. Activity branching picked
    the variables of defines_var annotations (sums, shortest-path values,
    the objective) as often as the model's decision variables, though they
    follow from those; it now branches on them only once every other
    variable is fixed. On the 42 instances of road-cons, city-position,
    gbac, celar, vrp, spot5 and trucking (60 s) the objective is better on
    32 and worse on 4: celar 1.4x to 3.5x, gbac about 2x, city-position
    1.5x to 2x. Trucking solved as a satisfaction problem finds a solution
    in 0.02 s, where it found none in 10 s. -Djacop.lcg.late=none turns it
    off.

34. Shorter linear explanations for lazy clause generation (--lcg). A
    lifted linear reason spends its slack on the terms that cost least
    first, so it leaves out as many terms as the slack allows, and the
    entailment reasons of reified linear constraints are lifted too. The
    FlatZinc front end splits a linear constraint of more than 40 terms
    into sums of blocks of about sqrt(n) terms, each with a new variable
    (--free-config lcg-split=<n>, 0 = off): its reasons cite about sqrt(n)
    bounds instead of n. On cutstock type10_TEST9 the demand sums' reasons
    go from 841 to 17 atoms and learnt clauses from 574 to 19; on vrp,
    spot5, gbac and cutstock (60 s, --lcg -f) more instances end better
    than worse.

35. Binpacking with cardinality also reasons on groups of consecutive
    bins: the items whose bins all lie in the group are packed in it, its
    count and load bounds are the sums over its bins, and the rules of one
    bin (counts, "too small", "too big", load bounds) are applied to the
    group. Each bin of a group can be fillable on its own while the group
    is not, as in curriculum models where precedences confine courses to
    ranges of periods. When the group rules keep pruning nothing they are
    run at fewer and fewer calls until they prune again. On 40 generated
    balanced academic curriculum instances (60 s) JaCoP proves all 40
    optima, against 37 before, and bacp_p10_n71_s0_1_6 needs 42 failures
    instead of 251,141; the minizinc-benchmarks bacp, curriculum and gbac
    instances end as before.

36. Free search (-f) for optimisation spends about one failure in twenty on
    objective probes, after OR-Tools CP-SAT's objective_lb_search and
    objective_shaving workers: runs with the objective bounded by its lower
    bound plus an offset (0 at first, doubled after each refuted probe,
    halved after a probe that reached its fail limit). A probe that
    explores its whole tree raises the lower bound at the root for good;
    probes do not teach dom/wdeg, give no restart nogoods and do not count
    as restarts. FreeSearch.Builder.probeObjective(share, failLimit) in the
    Java API (off by default), --free-config probe=<share> (0 = off),
    probe-fails=<n> in FlatZinc, and the statistics objectiveProbes,
    objectiveProbesRefuted and objectiveProbeFailures with -s. On 375
    optimisation instances of the free-search benchmark sets (60 s) it
    proves 6 more optima and loses 2, is 5 to 11 % faster on the
    instances both prove in three of the four sets (linear-to-program
    60 s -> 11 s, road-cons 60 s -> 1 s), and ends with a better objective
    on 24 of the others and a worse one on 21.

37. New constraint SubcircuitCostPair(x, c1, s1, c2, s2): two sums of arc
    costs on one subcircuit, such as a prize collected within a length
    budget, bounded together by Lagrangian relaxation: for lambda >= 0,
    s1 >= min assignment of (c1 + lambda * c2) - lambda * max(s2), and the
    same for the other sides. It searches lambda on the concave piecewise
    linear bound, prunes arcs by reduced costs, and backs off a direction
    that prunes nothing. The FlatZinc front end weighs the sum of a
    redundant SubcircuitCost against each other sum on the subcircuit
    (also a prize made of tests only), except under --lcg;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.circuitCostPair=true or false decides. On mario
    (MiniZinc challenge) the default search proves the optimum 3x faster
    (geometric mean of 5 instances, medium_4 32 s to 3 s) and -f stays
    within 0.5 s; on the 6 hardest orienteering instances proved in 30 s
    it is 1.4x faster (1.3x with -f), with an extra proof on
    orienteering_40_1.

38. A LinearInt that drops its fixed variables restores that state on
    backtracking only at the levels where it changed, instead of being
    called at every removed level. Many short sums, such as the blocks of
    a long sum split under --lcg, no longer slow down backtracking:
    skill_allocation_mzn_1m_2 (-f --lcg) solves in 11.9 s instead of
    17.2 s with the same search. Store.recordChange(Stateful) is the new
    hook for such state.

39. Not-last in CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive) now tests every
    task, as Vilim's algorithm does. A task whose latest completion lets
    no new task into Theta was skipped, and a task bounded by its own
    latest start now gets the largest latest start of the other tasks.
    Pruning is stronger, so search trees can change: the job shop and
    open shop instances solved to optimality keep their node counts, and
    a 150x15 job shop reaches makespan 8992 in 30 s instead of 9003.

40. Faster FlatZinc front end and input order tie-breaking. SimpleSelect
    breaks ties in input order from an int array kept in step with its
    search array instead of two map lookups per comparison; removing
    aliased variables from the default search is linear instead of
    quadratic; table rows are sorted and deduplicated as int[] without
    boxing; and each FlatZinc constraint name is resolved to its
    generator method once. oocsp_racks_100_r1 runs in 18 s instead of
    29 s (search 5.5 s instead of 15 s), with the same search tree.

41. --lcg -f takes turns at restarts between three kinds of runs: the
    search annotation (dom/wdeg without one), the SAT solver's own decisions
    on literals with phase false and phase saving, as in CP-SAT's default
    search, and activity branching. Before, it alternated the annotation
    with activity branching only. On the 258 vs-ortools instances (20 s) it
    proves 105 instead of 100 and answers 212 instead of 207, with the
    objective better on 28 and worse on 29. Among the instances where CP-SAT
    was far ahead (60 s): gbac UD8 4,793 -> 102, UD4 5,664 -> 1,557,
    reduced_UD3 10,790 -> 2,489; city-position 5-06 772,299 -> 36,376, 5-08
    420,538 -> 6,147, 8-06 605,806 -> 117,741; celar is 10 to 45 % worse.
    --free-config lcg-branching= selects literals (decisions on literals
    only), auto (literals, with activity branching from 1000 conflicts to a
    first solution: city-position 5-05 2,850 and road_17 12 million instead
    of 99 million, but it loses first solutions on cargo, linear-to-program
    and p1f) or activity (the previous search). With them, the sums of
    lcg-split are branched on last, as defined variables, and defined 0/1
    variables (reifications) are no longer branched on last: on
    skill-allocation 5d_1, where all but 126 of 12,851 variables are defined
    reifications, activity branching proves the optimum in 5 s instead of
    no proof in 30 s. Under --lcg, abs(x) = y with y >= k prunes the bounds
    of x instead of each value of -k+1..k-1, which made one atom per value,
    and the semantic minimisation of learnt clauses takes linear time on a
    long group of literals on one variable (city-position 7-04 learnt
    clauses of 7,527 literals).

42. Parallel portfolio for FlatZinc: fzn-jacop -p <n> (minizinc -p) solves
    the model in n threads, each with a store of its own: the options
    given, then free search and lazy clause generation with other settings
    (up to 9 threads). The threads share the objective value of the best
    solution, which FreeSearch reads at every node and LcgSolver adds as a
    clause, and the first thread that completes decides the answer. With
    -f -p 4 on 4 cores, 60 s: 52 of the 63 lost-free instances solved
    instead of 39 and 156 of 160 regression instances instead of 144. All
    solutions of satisfaction problems (-a, -n), -o and float models stay
    on one thread. New: FreeSearch.Builder.sharedBound, LcgSolver
    .setSharedBound and stop, CdclSolver.cancel, Store.interrupt.

43. FlatZinc objective presolve expands the objective through more of the
    model: equalities (bool2int, bool_eq, a 0/1 variable and its literal
    of 1) lead a term to an equal variable that has a definition; a
    disjunction r <-> or(b) (array_bool_or, bool_clause_reif) is
    r = sum(b) when at most one b can be 1 (literals of one variable, or
    of one value for variables of one jacop_alldiff/jacop_alldistinct);
    an element y = c[x] of constants is y = sum(c_v * (x = v)) when every
    value of x has a literal. On rel2onto each matched attribute now pays
    for its leaf edge at the root (5_5: bound 153,163 instead of 33,163,
    optimum 161,783). All six instances are proved in every mode (60 s):
    by default in 0.4 to 5.7 s instead of 1.9 to 26 s, with --lcg in 0.5
    to 1.4 s (2 of 6 proved before), with --lcg -f in 0.1 to 5.3 s (1 of
    6) and with -f in 0.1 to 6.9 s (3 of 6). On the 552 instances of the
    free-search kit only rel2onto and one prize-collecting instance
    change, with the same result.

44. Under --lcg a failure of element with a constant list is explained by
    the value's atoms when that takes fewer atoms than the index's: one
    atom of the value covers every position of its constant. On
    crosswords grid-19.05 dict-80 a failure took about 6,000 atoms and
    now takes 273 on average, and --lcg -f finds solutions there (550 in
    60 s, none before). Linear reasons of a bound that skipped holes now
    cite only the holes next to the bound, not every hole of the
    variable.

45. IntVar keeps the bounds of its domain in two int fields, as Gecode's
    IntVarImp does, so min(), max(), singleton() and value() no longer
    test the domain class and load from the domain. The fields are updated
    on every domain event other than ANY and on backtracking; search is
    unchanged (same node counts). Paired runs, 10 s: on the 100-instance
    dev tier (8 reps) the default search is 8.7% faster (geometric mean),
    -f 17.6% and --lcg 3.3%; on mznbench (249 instances, 3 reps) 8.5%,
    13.6% and 1.5%. Code that changes a variable's domain in place other
    than through the domain's in methods, addDom or setDomain (for example
    x.domain.subtractAdapt(v) before search) must call x.refreshBounds().

46. LCG free search (--lcg -f) can take turns between deciding the 0/1
    variables first and one order of activity for all variables: with
    --free-config lcg-booleans-first=turns its runs change between the two
    orders at each restart (on, the default, and off fix one order, as
    does -Djacop.lcg.booleansFirst). Each order wins clearly on some
    models (without: smelt, java-routing, jobshop swv07, on-call-rostering;
    with: community-detection, mario), and no static feature of the model
    predicted which. On the 249 mznbench instances of the free-search kit
    (60 s, lcg-split=0, two runs each), turns solved 161 and 159 against
    156 and 159 with Booleans first, and was better on 23 instances and
    worse on 10 in both runs. With lcg-split=40 it loses celar graph05,
    whose core-guided phase stalls without Booleans first, so it is not
    the default yet. New: LcgSolver.setBooleansFirst,
    setBooleansFirstTurns and the statistics lcgBooleanVariables,
    lcgWideVariables and lcgBooleansFirst.

47. --lcg -f no longer spends half of the time on core-guided search when
    branch and bound would prove the optimum at once. The core-guided
    phase now runs for 2% of the time, then branch and bound for 5%, then
    the rest of the core-guided phase, picking up where it stopped. The
    branch and bound in between bounds the objective by an assumption, not
    a clause, so the core-guided strata stay easy. nfc 18_3_10 takes 1.2 s
    instead of 25 s, fast-food ff36 1.5 s instead of 16 s, amaze 1 to 3 s
    instead of 15 to 31 s. On 97 instances (60 s, the OR-Tools set, a
    Challenge sample and the slow easy ones) the score is 53.0 against
    40.0, with 60 proofs against 58 and none lost (--free-config
    lcg-core-probe=<share>, lcg-core-delay=<share>, 0 = core-guided search
    first).

48. Optional ahead-of-time cache for fzn-jacop (Java 25, JEP 483/514/515).
    The new script fzn-jacop-aot (or mvn -Paot install) trains the JVM on
    the FlatZinc models in jacop-all/target/aot-training/ through the new
    org.jacop.fz.AotTraining and writes jacop.aot; fzn-jacop uses it when
    it is newer than the jar. It saves about 0.2 s of JVM start-up and
    class loading per run: on 54 small mznbench instances the median run
    with --lcg -f is 30 % shorter with the same search. Without the cache
    nothing changes. fzn-jacop now sends JVM warnings to stderr, so they
    cannot mix with the FlatZinc output.

49. --lcg -f on a model without a search annotation that has cumulative or
    disjunctive resources (also those found from disjunctions, as in job
    shop) branches on the start times of their tasks by set times in the
    runs that used dom/wdeg: the task that can start first starts as early
    as it can. dom/wdeg found a first makespan of 3803 on jobshop la17
    (optimum 784) and branch and bound needed 273 solutions. On 36 job shop
    instances (60 s) 29 are proved instead of 17. The statistic
    lcgSetTimesStarts gives the number of start variables.

50. Under --lcg, a lazy explanation that is not kept as a learnt clause
    (more than 3 literals) goes on a reason stack beside the clause
    arena, freed when its literal is unassigned, instead of a temporary
    arena clause that only a full arena compaction (learnt clauses and
    watch lists too) could reclaim. Conflicts from the CP side no longer
    allocate a boolean[] of the whole trail nor box literals to remove
    repeats. Learnt clauses are the same. Paired runs at 20 s: --lcg on
    carpet-cutting 17% faster per failure and the dev tier unchanged
    overall; --lcg -f 3% faster on the mznbench kit (also with
    --free-config lcg-split=0).

51. Under --lcg, CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive, and unary
    resources found from disjunctions) explains the bounds its rules give
    a start time by the tasks that must run before (or after) the task:
    detectable precedences, edge finding and not-first, with each bound
    lifted to the weakest atom on the trail the rule needs. Before, the
    time-table and energy explanations of CumulativeBasic could not derive
    detectable precedences at all, so many reasons were generic (48% on
    openshop j7per10-1, 290 atoms each) and learnt clauses ran to
    thousands of literals. --lcg -f, 30 s, 36 job shop instances: 27
    proved instead of 23, 21 better and 4 worse; openshop j7per10-1 and
    gp10-4 proved in 4 runs of 4 (2 of 4 before).
    -Djacop.lcg.unaryExplain=false turns it off.

52. Steadier --lcg -f measurements. The seed of -r now reaches LCG search
    (LcgSolver.setSeed): it changes the initial activity order of the
    variables and of the literals, so a configuration can be measured with
    several seeds. Constraint scopes are LinkedHashSets, so the order in
    which constraints are attached to their variables no longer follows
    identity hash codes: --lcg -f gave different searches with and without
    -Xshare:off (gfd-schedule n50f7d40m10k4: 53,045 and 24,313 nodes) and
    now gives one. --free-config lcg-keep=1 (LcgSolver.setKeepImprovingRun)
    lets a run that improved the best solution keep its way of branching at
    the next restart, as on city-position, where the large improvements
    come from one run descending from a solution and the restart rotated
    away from it. On 92 mznbench instances (60 s, seeds 0 to 2) it halved
    the spread of the final objectives between seeds, on develop-5.0 and
    with the Booleans-first turns, but Borda moved +2.2 and -3.0, so it
    is off by default. Scripts and runs: osolpro benchmarks/lcg-variance.

53. The dom/wdeg variable selection of free search (-f) is incremental.
    The unfixed search variables and the unfixed variables of the
    constraints are kept in reversible sparse sets, each constraint
    counts its unfixed variables, and for the default weighting the
    weighted degree of each variable is kept up to date, so a node no
    longer rescans every variable and the scopes of its constraints. The
    search is the same: same scores and the same ties (the variable
    given first). On the 652 instances of the dev tier and the
    free-search kit under -f (10 s, paired runs) the nodes per second
    rise by 15% in geometric mean over the 358 instances with at least
    1 s of search; on the dev tier with 8 repetitions by 7%, with no
    instance slower by more than 5%.

54. The parallel portfolio (-p) shares the best solution, not only its
    objective value: a free search thread whose best solution is worse
    replays it in a short run (100 failures) that fixes the model's
    variables to its values, so that its large neighbourhoods and value
    phases start from the portfolio's best solution. The thread of the
    options given without -f (depth-first search) now takes the shared
    objective bound too. With -f -p 4 on 4 cores, 60 s, against the
    portfolio without sharing: 23 instances better and 6 worse of the 160
    regression instances (time of proofs x0.77), 14 better and 6 worse of
    the 63 lost-free ones. New: FreeSearch.Builder.sharedSolution,
    SharedSolution, DepthFirstSearch.setSharedBound; -s prints
    portfolioImports.
    
55. Store.watchedConstraints (a public map from variables to the constraints
    that watch them as watched literals) is removed; the store keeps these
    constraints in the variable. Use registerWatchedLiteralConstraint and
    deregisterWatchedLiteralConstraint as before, and the new
    watchedLiteralConstraints(v) and watchedLiteralConstraints() to read them.
    Waking them, and finding the position of a woken variable in Nooverlap,
    LinearBool, LexOrder, LexChain, ExtensionalSupportStr and the restart
    nogoods of free search, no longer goes through a HashMap (new
    org.jacop.util.VarIntMap). Search is unchanged; free search (-f) is about
    2.5% faster on mznbench.

56. The linear relaxation (--lp) adds cutting planes at its first solve,
    in the root propagation: up to 5 rounds of mixed-integer rounding
    (MIR) cuts, from the rows of the basis inverse of the basic columns
    with fractional values (Gomory mixed-integer cuts) and from the rows
    themselves. Each cut is the MIR of an integer combination of the
    rows, computed exactly in long arithmetic with the bounds of that
    moment, so it holds for the whole search and is explained under
    --lcg like the other rows; the floating-point simplex only proposes
    it. DualSimplex adds the rows and keeps its basis. A round that does
    not move the bound is dropped again; -Dorg.jacop.lp.cuts=false turns
    cuts off. The first solve may take 20,000 pivots
    (-Dorg.jacop.lp.rootPivots) instead of 500, within the root work
    budget. Fixes an overflow in the propagation of a combination of rows
    whose slack exceeded a long, which pruned values of solutions. On 46
    of the 422 vs-ortools instances that the change touches (20 s), proofs
    go from 24 to 29 (default), 23 to 28 (--lcg) and 25 to 30 (-f), with
    no proof lost: trucking 06, 07, 09 and 11 are proved in 1-5 s (06,
    07 and 11 also under --lcg), and the cuts lift the root bound of
    trucking 09 from 596 to the optimum 871.
    New statistics lpRootBound, lpCuts, lpCutBound.

57. Under --lcg, Cumulative explains two more kinds of pruning itself
    instead of with the generic reason. Time-table disjunctive reasoning
    (on by default in the FlatZinc front end) pushes a task j past a task
    i that every placement of j would overlap where the compulsory parts
    of the other tasks leave less than r_i + r_j; when no energy window
    explains a start, the reason is the bounds that keep i on two points
    (or an interval), j's bounds and the compulsory parts there. Upper
    bounds of variable durations and resource uses are explained by the
    time table, looking past values already out of the domain (a resource
    use in {0, 9} capped at 2 is recorded as <= 0). Edge finding and
    time-table edge finding were already explained by the energy windows.
    On the 76 instances with a cumulative in the free-search sets, generic
    Cumulative reasons fall from 0.79% (--lcg) and 5.05% (--lcg -f) to 0,
    and those of CumulativeUnary from 4.53% to 0.78% and 0.82% to 0.03%.
    Paired runs: --lcg 60 s 8 better and 3 worse, --lcg -f 30 s with
    seeds 1 to 3 16 better and 10 worse. -Djacop.lcg.cumulativePairs=false
    turns the pair reasons off. Scripts and runs: osolpro
    benchmarks/cumulative-explanations.

58. Under --lcg, JaSAT stays in stable mode (Luby restarts) once its
    first focused phase of 1,000 conflicts is over, instead of
    alternating the two modes. Restarts become far rarer, and each one
    costs the CP propagation back down from level 0. Paired runs, 60 s,
    --lcg -f on the 123 mznbench instances with at least 2,000 conflicts:
    23 better and 13 worse, 59 complete instead of 54; --lcg: 4 better,
    1 worse. The other 126 instances barely reach stable mode.
    -Djacop.lcg.stableForGood=false restores the alternation.

59. JaSAT misses no unit propagation when CP drives it. Under --lcg and
    -sat, a clause added during search, a learnt clause asserted by the
    external search, or a propagator's reason kept as a clause could have
    its true watch above its false watch's level; after a backtrack between
    the two levels it was unit and never visited (under --lcg even at level
    0). Under -sat, units asserted again by a backtrack lost their
    implications once the next level was opened. CdclSolver.setChecking
    (-Djacop.jasat.check=true) now checks at every fixpoint that no clause
    is false or unit and that every reason is valid. On 125 mznbench
    instances (60 s) --lcg solves 68 instead of 67 and --lcg -f 77 as
    before, with no measurable cost.

60. LCG explanations of Regular (and the other layered graph constraints)
    cite only the edges that lead to the removed values: the nearest cut
    or the cut nearest the ends of the graph, whichever is shorter
    (Gange, Stuckey and Szymanek, Constraints 2011), instead of every
    edge leaving the reachable nodes. Reasons on nonogram fall from 15.4
    to 3.8 atoms. On the 92 regular/mdd instances of the free-search
    kit and jacop-benchmarks (60 s, paired), --lcg solves 66 instead of
    60 and --lcg -f 72 instead of 66. -Djacop.lcg.minimality=N measures
    every N-th explanation: with -s, the %% lcg-min lines give per
    constraint class how many atoms propagation needs and how many could
    be weaker.

61. Lifted LCG explanations (linear, cumulative, subcircuit) may cite a
    bound weaker than any trail atom when the solver already has a true
    literal for it, set before the explained one. On mznbench (249,
    60 s, paired) --lcg is better on 15 instances and worse on 6
    (evilshop js-brs2-7 proved optimal, mrcpsp j30_1_10 proved UNSAT,
    rcpsp-max j30_169 26 s to 4 s); --lcg -f is level (32 to 32).
    -Djacop.lcg.liftLiterals=false turns it off; %%%mzn-stat
    lcgLiftedLiterals counts the literals cited.

62. IncreasingSum, LexOrder and CountVar (also under Reified and Implies)
    explain their own pruning under --lcg instead of the generic or
    replayed reason. IncreasingSum cites the bounds of the order and the
    sum, LexOrder the bounds of the prefix that is forced equal or
    greater and of the position that decides, CountVar the variables
    counted and those whose domains miss the value. Generic reasons fall
    from 876,758 to 0 on primes_param and from 209,374 to 0 on
    amaze_mod; LexOrder reasons on mqueens from 129 to 21 atoms. Paired
    60 s runs: --lcg better on 10 instances and worse on 1 (mqueens n11
    6 s to 4 s, mqueens n20 solved); --lcg -f is within noise on the
    order and sum instances (a rerun of the 12 that differed split 4
    to 4) and better on 4 of 8 amaze instances (amaze 2012-03-15 39 s
    to 1.4 s).

63. Shorter Cumulative explanations under --lcg. Explaining a start bound
    walks over the starts it removed; where those starts had been removed
    already, it cited one removal per value, so on carpet-cutting some
    reasons had 300 to 600 atoms. A compulsory part that rules out the
    same starts is now cited instead when it takes fewer atoms. Energy
    window reasons keep only the tasks the window's excess needs, each
    with its start bounds lifted to the overlap it must have. carpet-
    cutting test.20: 59 to 10 atoms per Cumulative reason (--lcg), 75 to
    10 (--lcg -f). Paired runs against item 56 alone: --lcg 60 s 8 better
    and 1 worse, --lcg -f 30 s with seeds 1 to 3 34 better and 11 worse,
    with new proofs on rcpsp_08, smelt_3 and rcpsp-wet j60_36_8.
    -Djacop.lcg.windowLift=false keeps the full window reasons.

64. New decomposed constraint VectorBinpacking(bin, load, w, minBin) for
    vector (multidimensional) bin packing: one Binpacking per dimension,
    a Binpacking with cardinality rules on the surrogate dimension (the
    sum of the dimensions scaled to a common capacity, Caprara and Toth
    2001), and an Alldistinct on each clique of the conflict graph (items
    that exceed the capacity of some dimension together, Gualandi and
    Lombardi, CP 2013). The FlatZinc front end posts the surrogate
    dimension for bin_packing constraints over the same bin variables, one
    per dimension (-Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingSurrogate=false turns it
    off); the cliques are off by default
    (-Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingCliques=vector, or all, turns them on). On
    75 random vector bin packing instances (30 s) the surrogate dimension
    proved 62 optima instead of 49 with the model's search, 65 instead of
    54 with free search and 59 instead of 44 with free search and --lcg;
    the cliques proved no more.

65. Presolve breaks value symmetries and orbitopes too (--presolve on|check,
    unless --symmetry off), in org.jacop.fz.SymmetryBreaking:
    - Value precedence. Integer variables whose values are only compared
      (int_eq/int_ne and x - y = 0 or != 0, all_different, all_equal,
      count, at least, at most, member, nvalue, element, gcc and bin packing
      with their value-indexed counts and loads) form a value space; groups
      with the same values form one space, as the machines of jobs that no
      constraint joins. A second graph joins the space's variables to
      vertices of their values and constants in those arguments become the
      value vertices; the generators that fix the variables permute the
      values, and a set of values they permute in every way (connected
      transpositions, or a Schreier-Sims check up to 40 values) is posted
      as one SeqPrecedeChain over the space's variables, which then come
      first in the lex-leader order of the variable symmetries. A
      minimised objective that only bounds the space from above (x <= z,
      z = max(x)), as the number of colours, is a dominance that keeps
      the precedence and the optimum.
    - Orbitopes. Generators that swap two columns of variables, row by
      row, and chain into connected swaps of columns are replaced by one
      LexChain over the columns when the columns fit the lex-leader order.
      GraphAutomorphisms now prefers transpositions, which finds them.
    On 140 generated instances (graph colouring, fewest colours, bin
    packing with integer and 0/1 models, P||Cmax, jobs on identical
    machines) at 30 s, solved instances go from 74 to 93 with the
    annotation search, 84 to 93 with -f and 89 to 93 with --lcg -f; bin
    packing from 0 to 10 with the annotation search. --lcg -f loses 2
    P||Cmax instances to a different search. Detection takes a median of
    0.05 s on one model of each directory of jacop-benchmarks; value
    precedence fires on 38 of 1,926 and orbitopes on 101.

66. Fewer SAT variables under --lcg. A value a constraint removes from
    inside a domain is now a transient [x = v] literal, released on
    backtracking unless a clause keeps it, as bound literals already were;
    before, each was linked to two new bound literals by four clauses.
    CumulativeUnary's time table no longer cuts holes into start domains
    under --lcg (the sweep of CumulativeBasic already did not): jobshop
    swv07 made 357k SAT variables in 20 s, now 48k. Annotation decisions
    x = min and x = max are taken as [x <= min] and [x >= max], without
    the [x = v] literal, and the annotation's variable selection starts
    from the position it reached at the decision level instead of the
    first variable. New statistics lcgBoundLiterals, lcgEqualityLiterals,
    lcgTransientLiterals, lcgAuxiliaryVariables, lcgSatVariables,
    lcgSatConflicts and lcgSatPropagations. Paired runs on mznbench, 30 s:
    --lcg 19 better / 3 worse, --lcg -f 50 / 52 (two runs; noise level),
    with 15% fewer SAT variables at the median.

67. Dead code removed (about 3,300 lines, no change in behaviour). Classes
    nothing used: IntervalBasedBacktrackableManager and
    SimpleBacktrackableManager (the store always uses
    TrailBacktrackableManager), SwitchesPruningLogging (no-op tracing
    stubs), LengauerTarjan and SophisticatedLengauerTarjan, TableMill,
    geost ShiftOrder and BoxDisplay (an AWT debug view behind a constant
    false switch), AbstractArithmeticConstraint and AbstractXplusYeq.
    Unused methods of the core interfaces: Domain.isNumeric(),
    noConstraints(), noSearchConstraints() and searchConstraints(),
    IntDomain.elementsSmallerThan(int), Var.afcValue(),
    Var.sizeSearchConstraints(), the Function overloads of
    Var.positionMapping and addPositionMapping, MutableVar.previous() and
    setCurrent(), MutableVarValue.previous(),
    BacktrackableManager.isRecognizedAsChanged(int), and in Store
    throwFailException(Var), clearWatchedConstraint(),
    countConstraint(int) and the field auxilaryVariables.
    The constraint replacement feature is removed too: nothing registered
    a replacement, yet Store.impose looked one up for every constraint.
    Store.registerReplacement and deregisterReplacement, the interface
    org.jacop.api.Replaceable and org.jacop.constraints.replace.ReifiedIfThen
    are gone.

66. SeqPrecedeChain (seq_precede_chain and value_precede_chain, the value
    precedence that presolve posts) and LexChain (the orbitope chains)
    explain their own pruning under --lcg instead of the generic or
    replayed reason. SeqPrecedeChain cites, for a value of rank above s,
    the removals of the value of rank s before its position (no earlier
    position can reach rank s), and for a forced position the domain that
    forces a rank and the removals of each rank's value between its latest
    position and its deadline. LexChain cites the vectors of one side of
    the chain: the smallest tuples from the first vector, or the largest
    from the last, bound the vector they reach. Failures are explained the
    same way. On a P||Cmax instance value precedence reasons fall from 22
    to 8 atoms, and none is generic or replayed (before: 38% generic, the
    rest replayed). On 25 jacop-benchmarks
    instances with value precedence or orbitopes, --lcg -f at 30 s solves
    22 instead of 20, and the 130 generated colouring, bin packing and
    identical-machine instances are level (83 to 82 solved).

63. NetworkFlow explains its pruning under --lcg instead of the generic
    reason (networks with constant arc costs). The cost bound and the
    arc bounds come from the duals of a minimum cost flow over the
    bounds before the explained atom, which certify that every flow
    costs at least sum_a min(rc l, rc u) - sum_v p b; a reason cites
    the arc bounds this needs, weakened within the slack. Feasibility
    pruning is explained by the cut that the flow search stops at, and
    the cost's upper bound by the fixed flows. On mapping, reasons fall
    from 23 to 5 atoms and generic ones to 0. Paired 60 s runs on the
    30 instances with jacop_networkflow: --lcg better on 7 and worse
    on none (mesh2x2_mp3 3.1 s to 1.6 s), --lcg -f better on 8 and
    worse on 1 (mesh2x2_mp3 4.4 s to 1.6 s).

68. Several objectives, lexicographic or Pareto. New class
    org.jacop.search.MultiObjective optimises integer objectives (each
    minimised or maximised) as a sequence of single-objective
    subproblems: lexicographic() optimises them in order, each with the
    earlier ones fixed at their optimum and required to be strictly
    better than the best solution so far; pareto() returns the Pareto
    front, each point optimised that way among the solutions no point
    found before dominates, with the later objectives bounded by the best
    solution so far, so every point found is Pareto optimal and the front
    is complete when no solution is left. MultiObjective.DepthFirst
    solves the subproblems by branch and bound in a store. The FlatZinc
    front end reads MiniZinc's goal_hierarchy annotation
    (std/experimental.mzn: min_goal, max_goal, int_min_goal,
    int_max_goal, sat_goal) for the lexicographic optimum and JaCoP's
    pareto annotation (jacop.mzn) for the Pareto front, on a solve item
    with satisfy. Each subproblem is solved as a FlatZinc model with the
    options given (-f, --lcg, --presolve, -p apply to it) and -t covers
    them all. Pareto points are printed as they are proven, followed by
    ========== when the front is complete. Float goals are not supported.

68. New constraints CostAlldifferent(x, costs, cost, offset) and
    CostGCC(x, costs, counters, cost, offset): all-different and global
    cardinality with a cost matrix (SICStus all_different/2 and
    global_cardinality/3 with cost(C, M)), cost = sum(i)(costs[i][x[i]]).
    Both use the incremental Hungarian method of CircuitCost, now for
    more values than variables and, for CostGCC, one column per allowed
    copy of a value (counter upper bounds; lower bounds are left out of
    the relaxation). The minimum and maximum assignments bound the cost
    and remove values by reduced costs. The FlatZinc front end posts
    CostAlldifferent as a redundant constraint on all_different and
    inverse with cost = sum(i)(d[i, x[i]]), also when the sum has other
    terms (a new variable for the cost part and a linear constraint);
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.costAlldifferent=false turns it off. CostGCC is posted
    only with -Dorg.jacop.fz.costGcc=true: on the Challenge models with
    such a GCC (chessboard, blocks-world, evm-super-compilation) it cost
    more time than it saved. On kidney-exchange (60 s) it proves one more
    instance and halves the time of another; rel2onto and seat-moving
    are unchanged.

68. Free search (-f) no longer depends on identity hash codes, so runs
    of the same model under other JVM flags (-Xshare:off, CDS or AOT
    caches, -XX:hashCode) or after unrelated class changes search the
    same tree. Besides the constraint scopes (item 52), the variables of
    the Boolean vector constraints (and_bool, or_bool, ...), the
    channeling constraints the FlatZinc front end collects from
    int_eq_reif and its alias equalities were hash sets or maps keyed by
    variables, so constraints were attached and posted in a different
    order: zephyrus 12_6_8_3 took 107777, 106436 or 113314 nodes to prove
    its optimum. Adaptive LNS under -f still shares its time by the wall
    clock (see item 69).

69. FlatZinc runs repeat their search from run to run of one build.
    Without -r the seed is 0 (it was drawn by the JVM), so the
    neighbourhoods of adaptive LNS, indomain_random and on_restart
    random values repeat. Under -f, runs in neighbourhoods take their
    share (lns-share, now 0.8 by default) of the propagations of the
    other runs, not of their time; by propagations a share of 1 gave
    them more time than before and lost 3 to 4 proofs per seed on 92
    mznbench instances, 0.8 is level with the old split (Borda 46.3 :
    45.7 over 3 seeds, 30 s). Under --lcg, the
    replays of a constraint that explain its pruning take at most
    jacop.lcg.replayShare (0.25) of the consistency calls the store made
    for it (Constraint.consistencyCalls()), not 25% of the time, which
    made rel2onto 5_17 take 2759 to 2770 nodes. Before, 2DBinPacking
    Class7_40_6 under -f took 3,494, 29,933 or 12,964 nodes in 20 s and
    now 22,488 every time. With a time limit, the phases of --lcg -f
    (branch and bound, core-guided search, neighbourhood runs) still
    share the time by the clock: by conflicts, the neighbourhood runs
    took 1.3 to 7.7 times the time of branch and bound instead of 3.

70. Under --lcg, XplusYeqZ keeps to bounds when x or y is fixed instead of
    copying the holes of z into the other term: each copied hole was a
    value-removal atom, a SAT variable of its own. On CELAR6-SUB4
    (frequency differences) that made 280k SAT variables in 30 s and cut
    the conflict rate threefold; now 29k, 3.3x the conflicts, and
    celar_scen06 under --lcg -f reaches 4,502 instead of 9,531 in 30 s.
    Paired mznbench runs, 30 s: --lcg -f 24 better / 21 worse (noise
    level apart from celar), --lcg 8 / 4. Without --lcg nothing changes.

71. Under --lcg, Binpacking explains its cardinality rules (card
    variables, as the FlatZinc front end adds for gbac and bacp, or
    cardinalityReasoning) instead of leaving them to the generic
    reason: the bounds of the card variables, items kept out of a bin
    or a group of consecutive bins that is full or that they would
    overload or underload, items a bin or group needs, and loads from
    the lightest or heaviest items its count allows. On gbac UD5 the
    generic share of Binpacking's reasons drops from 43% to 0.01% and
    the average reason from 33 to 15 atoms. --lcg -f, 30 s, 2 runs on
    15 gbac instances: 9 better, 3 worse, mean objective 14% lower;
    bacp unchanged. -Djacop.lcg.binpackingCounts=false turns it off.

72. Under --lcg -f, the core-guided phase gives its time to branch and
    bound when it has no solution of its own after a third of its time
    (jacop.lcg.coreNoSolution), as intended. Before, a solution of the
    branch and bound run before the phase also counted, and there nearly
    always is one, so the phase ran its whole share: city-position 7-04
    spent half of its time in cores that raised the bound to about 450
    and ended at 69,360 in 30 s, and now ends at 3,286 (CP-SAT: 2,930).
    On 96 optimisation instances of the OR-Tools comparison set, 30 s, two
    paired runs: 29 better, 11 worse. -s prints lcgCoreNoSolutionStops;
    -Djacop.lcg.coreAnySolution=true restores the old test.

73. The linear relaxation (--lp) also reads constraints that are not
    linear relations (org.jacop.constraints.Linearizations): big-M rows
    of reified and half-reified linear constraints, and/or of Booleans,
    McCormick rows of products, the lower side of max/min, abs,
    alldifferent sum bounds, value encodings of a variable by
    Booleans (b_v <-> x = v gives sum b_v = 1 and x = sum v b_v) and
    element over an encoded index. These rows are added at the first
    solve and kept only when they raise the root bound, make the
    relaxation infeasible or are at most 100 (-Dorg.jacop.lp.smallExtra);
    a relaxation whose solves in search hit the pivot limit three times
    (and in one of twenty solves) goes back to the rows without them.
    -Dorg.jacop.lp.linearize=false turns them off. On the 195 of the 422
    vs-ortools instances whose relaxation they change (20 s, paired),
    proofs go from 82 to 87 (default), 114 to 117 (--lcg) and 108 to 114
    (-f): rcpsp-wet j30_27_5, j30_43_10 and j60_19_6, maximum-dag 25_01
    and 25_04, smelt_4. New statistics lpBaseBound, lpExtraRows,
    lpExtraDropped.

74. The FlatZinc front end merges the copies MiniZinc makes of the result
   of a partial function used in a reified context (to_enum, an array
   access whose index may be out of range): each call site gets a fresh
   z with b -> z = x, c -> z = k and not b -> c. Copies with the same x,
   b and k become one variable and their three constraints are dropped;
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.partialCopies=false turns this off. A clause whose
   literals are all b <-> (x = v) or b -> (x = v) on one variable x now
   also restricts x to those values when the model is loaded (leaf \/
   unused written as (x = 10) \/ (x = 11) gives x in 10..11). On the
   MiniZinc Challenge 2020 minimal-decision-sets instances the search
   tree halves and a node is about 15% cheaper; backache_train4 and
   colic_train4 are now proved within 20 minutes.

75. New constraint MultiCumulative(starts, durations, heights, limits):
    several cumulative resources used by the same tasks with fixed
    durations, heights[r][i] being the use of resource r by task i (the
    cumulative resources of SICStus multi_cumulative). One time-table sweep
    keeps a profile per resource, so the task events are sorted once and
    the constraint wakes once per change instead of once per resource; it
    prunes what one time-table cumulative per resource prunes. The FlatZinc
    front end merges the cumulative constraints with fixed durations and
    resource uses that share tasks (a start variable that is not fixed,
    with the same duration) into one MultiCumulative; each of them keeps
    its energy and edge-finding rules (Cumulative.doTimeTable(false)) and
    runs them in the next queue. Not under --lcg, where each cumulative
    explains its own time table, nor with --presolve, whose cumulative
    reductions read each resource as one constraint;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.multiCumulative=false turns it off. On the 47 MiniZinc Challenge instances it applies to (rcpsp,
    racp, rcpsp-wet, cyclic-rcpsp, mspsp, smelt, aircraft-disassembly,
    ihtc), 60 s: +7.4% nodes per second with the model search (3 better
    objectives, 1 faster proof, none worse) and +6.4% with -f (results
    within run-to-run noise).

76. fzn-jacop (and minizinc --solver org.jacop) solves with lazy clause
    generation by default, with the model's search and with -f. --lcg now
    takes on|off ("--lcg off" gives the previous default; --lcg alone still
    turns it on), and -Djacop.lcg=off changes the default. Default LCG is
    left out where LCG is not used anyway: models with float variables,
    satisfaction problems asking for more than one solution (-a, -n), -sat,
    and models loaded through FlatzincLoader for the API. Presolve stays off
    by default. On 249 MiniZinc Challenge instances (60 s) LCG proves 32
    more instances with the model's search (143 against 111, Borda 128
    against 90) and 19 more with -f, where Borda is even with plain free
    search; with -f it also proves 8 more of the 63 instances free search
    used to lose. Presolve on, also without probing and symmetry breaking,
    lost Borda in every configuration measured.

77. --lcg no longer runs out of memory on large alldiffs. Each value a
    constraint removes from inside a domain was a SAT variable, and an
    alldiff over 4096 variables of 4096 values removes 8.4 million
    values on one branch (debruijn_binary_04_06 filled a 3 GB heap in
    40 s while plain search solves it in 10 s). Past a literal budget,
    one SAT variable per KB of maximum heap (-Djacop.lcg.literalBudget),
    a removal with no literal of its own stays out of the SAT solver,
    and a clause that would cite it cites its explanation instead. The
    SAT solver also makes a literal's watch lists when a clause first
    watches it rather than up front. debruijn_binary_04_06 now solves
    in 46 s with -Xmx3G, with --lcg and with --lcg -f; below the budget
    nothing changes.

78. New constraint OrBoolWatched(list, result, negated): result is the
    disjunction of 0/1 variables (or its negation), propagated with value
    watches as the two watched literals of a clause, so a variable fixed
    to 0 that is not watched does not wake it. The FlatZinc front end
    posts int_lin_le_reif(sum(x) <= 0, b) over 8 or more Booleans with it
    instead of Not(OrBoolVector) (not under --lcg;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.orBoolWatchMin sets the length). It visits the same
    search tree; on pattern-set-mining the default search runs 1.6-3.9x
    more nodes per second and proves ionosphere in 35 s. A constraint
    that watches values can now count as a constraint of the watched
    variable (Constraint.countsValueWatch, Var.constraintsWithWatches),
    which dom/wdeg, the failure-count selections and LNS read.

79. The FlatZinc completion search, which labels the variables a search
    on output variables leaves out, labels them in input order instead of
    smallest domain first. It only checks that one assignment exists, so
    the solutions are the same, but choosing by domain size scanned every
    unfixed variable at every node: 70% of the run on freepizza, which
    now runs up to 14x more nodes (pizza39 reaches 942033 in 20 s
    instead of 972454).

80. The linear relaxation (--lp) costs less in search. The dense dual
    simplex reads its dual values from the reduced costs of the slack
    columns instead of a product over the whole basis, and updates the
    basic values four rows at a time in the same order. The search stays
    the same on the instances checked, and on java-routing trip_8_1 a
    solve takes 0.49 ms instead of 0.90 ms. A relaxation that deduces nothing now backs off to one
    solve in up to 4096 calls instead of 64 (-Dorg.jacop.lp.maxPeriod).
    Options given as --name=value, such as --lp=off, are read as
    --name value; before, they were ignored as unknown options.

81. Under --lcg, CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive) explains a
    failure that only its unary rules detect: a task that detectable
    precedences, edge finding or not-first push past its latest start.
    These failures got the generic reason, every start bound of the
    resource; on flexible-jobshop med04 that was 5,154 of 5,162 failures
    of the resource, and its learnt clauses fall from 458 to 24 atoms on
    average. On 40 scheduling instances with a disjunctive, 60 s, --lcg
    gets 5 better results and 1 worse (med04 2,960 to 2,643, evilshop
    js-setf3-10 now proved optimal), --lcg -f 7 better and 1 worse.

82. Under --lcg, nvalue (Values), at_most and at_least explain their
    pruning and failures instead of giving the generic reason. Values
    cites variables with pairwise disjoint intervals, or the different
    values of the fixed variables and variables whose domains miss them,
    as its propagator counts them; at_most and at_least cite the
    variables fixed to the value or without it, as count does. Count
    also explains a bound that skips holes next to the counted value.
    gfd-schedule n50f7d40m10k4 had 1.1 million generic reasons of 64
    atoms from these constraints and has none; at 30 s it ends at 611
    instead of 709 with --lcg and proves its optimum in 1.4 s instead of
    9.8 s with --lcg -f. On the 51 benchmark instances with nvalue,
    at_most, at_least or count, two paired 30 s runs: --lcg 5 better
    and 4 worse, --lcg -f 8 better and 3 worse.

83. Under --lcg, a linear bound that moves past a hole of its variable's
    initial domain wider than 4,096 values keeps its own reason. The
    explanation checked every integer between the bound the reason
    implies and the recorded one, and gave the generic reason past
    4,096; it now walks only the values of the initial domain. Variables
    over sparse values (the cubes of routing-flexible, liner-sf
    repositioning) hit this: routing_GCM_0006 had 110,000 generic linear
    reasons at 20 s and liner-sf tp7_0 37,000 of 187 atoms on average,
    and both have none. On 20 liner-sf, routing-flexible and java-routing
    instances, 30 s, two paired runs: --lcg 3 better and none worse
    (upTo1hour tp7_0 462,388 to 196,237), --lcg -f 9 better and 2 worse.

84. New constraint SymmetricAlldifferent(x, offset): x[i] = j + offset
    if and only if x[j] = i + offset, MiniZinc symmetric_all_different
    and inverse(x, x). A solution is a perfect matching of the graph
    whose edges are the pairs of variables that may take each other's
    index, found with Edmonds' blossom algorithm. It is domain
    consistent (one matching search per variable at each call);
    SymmetricAlldifferent(x, offset, false) only channels x[i] = j with
    x[j] = i and fails when no perfect matching exists, as with an odd
    number of variables and no fixed point. Under --lcg it explains a
    failure, and a removed value, with a Tutte barrier read from the
    failed search. The FlatZinc front end posts the second one for
    jacop_assignment(x, x, i, i), which MiniZinc's inverse(x, x) and
    the new fzn_symmetric_all_different give; before, it was Assignment
    (and Alldistinct under :: domain), which needs search to find an
    odd cycle. -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetricAlldifferent=domain selects the
    domain consistent one and =false Assignment.
    MiniZinc Challenge, default options, 60 s: p1f 2015 13, 15 and 17
    (UNSAT) fail at the root in under 1 s instead of 1.1-5.0 s of
    search; the 20 elitserien instances take 328 s instead of 323 s;
    the domain consistent version takes 412 s for the same objectives.

85. Local search (--free-config ls=<share>, off by default) makes
    feasibility jump moves (Luteberget and Sartor, 2023): each variable
    of a violated constraint has a jump value, the value that most
    lowers the weighted violation of all its constraints, tried at the
    bounds, next to its value and where each linear row starts or stops
    being violated; a step makes the best jump of a sample of variables,
    and at a minimum every violated constraint weighs more. Jumps are
    cached until one of the variable's constraints changes.
    LocalSearch.Builder.jump(true) selects them; ls-moves=walk restores
    the moves of one violated constraint per step. On 105 instances with
    a slow or no first solution, 30 s, local search itself finds a
    solution on 23 instances instead of 17 with -f --lcg off and on 17
    instead of 15 with --lcg -f (cutstock, cyclic-rcpsp, largescheduling
    and magicseq only with jumps).

86. FlatZinc if_then_else on integers is posed as the new constraint
    ConditionalSelect(b, x, y): y = x[k] for the first k with b[k] = 1.
    It keeps y within the bounds of the branches that can still be
    selected, rules out a branch that cannot give y its value and selects
    the only branch left, with its own LCG explanations. IfThenElse and
    Conditional over equalities, posted before, gave y no bound until the
    selecting condition was fixed. median-string (MiniZinc Challenge 2019)
    p2_10_8-0 is proved in 6 s instead of 38 s with -f, and with the
    model's search p2_2_20-2 and p2_4_20-0 are proved in 34 s and 38 s
    (not in 120 s, and in 113 s). On 62 Challenge instances with
    if_then_else, 60 s: with the model's search 12 objectives better and 3
    worse; with -f 8 proofs faster, and on two seeds 6 objectives better
    and 9 worse (ptv pax_period_20_1c, cgt and stripboard the largest).
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.conditionalSelect=false restores the old posting.

87. Under --lcg, an experimental cut-based conflict analysis, off by
    default: -Djacop.lcg.linear=learn. When a linear constraint fails,
    it is combined with the linear constraints behind its latest bounds,
    eliminating one variable at a time, until the sum is asserting; the
    sum is then posted as a learnt linear constraint (up to
    -Djacop.lcg.linearTerms=200 terms) next to the learnt clause.
    -Djacop.lcg.linear=stats only counts the outcomes (%% lcg-linear with
    -s). On the 249 mznbench instances only about 2.5% of failures end in
    such a sum. With 30 terms at most, 30 s: golomb_09 and ghoulomb
    4-9-10 prove 1.7x faster with 2.5x fewer conflicts, bus_scheduling r4
    takes 9.8 s instead of 17 s under --lcg and is proved under --lcg -f;
    over the whole set, --lcg 15 better and 6 worse, --lcg -f 13 better
    and 33 worse, mostly on instances where it never learns (noise).

88. A FlatZinc all_different over more than 100 variables with sparse
    domains (successor arrays) is posted as Alldistinct (domain
    consistency) instead of Alldiff (bounds consistency), which pruned
    nothing inside such domains. MiniZinc Challenge 2023
    multi-agent-graph-coverage rnd_n40_p1000_c20_s1 now has its first
    solution (4040, as SICStus) in 4 s and rnd_n75_p1000_c50_s1 (40474,
    as SICStus, the only fd entry with a solution) in 51 s; JaCoP found
    none in 20 minutes. Under --lcg, the all different explanation also
    walks only the values of each variable's initial domain instead of
    its whole span, and explains a value taken by a fixed variable by
    that variable's bounds without building the value graph: rnd_n75
    makes 50 times more conflicts per second.

89. New constraint MinOfSums(x, y, m): m = min(x[i] + y[i]), a term
    being x[i] alone where y[i] is null, with the bounds pruning of the
    decomposition z[i] = x[i] + y[i], m = min(z) and its own explanations
    under --lcg. The FlatZinc front end merges a sum z = x + y (int_lin_eq
    [1, 1, -1]) into the minimum (jacop_minimum, int_min) that is its only
    use, when z is not output, not in the solve item and its domain holds
    every value of x + y; -Dorg.jacop.fz.minOfSums=false turns this off.
    The shortest-path model of road-cons has one such sum for every pair
    of edges (130,000 of 138,000 variables on road_26), and every change
    of a sum woke both constraints. With --lcg off the search tree is the
    same and road_26 explores 1,350 instead of 207 nodes/s (Gecode 6.4.0
    372 on the same machine). Under --lcg, LcgSolver.setUnusedVariables
    keeps the merged sums out of branching. On the 10 road-cons
    instances, 60 s, default options: road_11 (above1hour) proves its
    optimum 5,020 in 17 s, road_33 and road_9 prove theirs 3.5 and 7
    times faster, and the other 7 end better (road_25 16,675 instead of
    22,133, road_26 2,027,861 instead of 22,018,497); with -f road_25
    reaches its optimum 15,453 in 20 s. Min, Max and XplusYeqZ (bounds
    case) read the bounds kept on IntVar and only call the domain when a
    bound changes, +7 to +11% on road-cons before the merge.

90. A FlatZinc variable declared with a bound beyond JaCoP's integer range
    (-536,870,910..536,870,909) is narrowed to it, with one warning on
    stderr; MiniZinc writes such computed bounds, for example
    var 0..4722438400 for a product in 2022 arithmetic-target. Before, a
    bound beyond 32 bits was a parser error and a bound between the range
    and 32 bits (arithmetic-target 0..1687566400, connect, code-generator,
    unit-commitment) gave a variable outside the range JaCoP supports.
    After narrowing, a completed search is reported as optimal only when
    the objective alone lost values, on its worse side, and is never
    reported as unsatisfiable. A domain wholly outside the range is
    rejected. All 3 failing arithmetic-target instances now run (60 s:
    4108 reaches the optimum 7; 6872 and 8657 reach 8 and 9 with -f,
    optimum 7); the two code-generator instances still prove their optimum.

91. Free search without lazy clause generation (--lcg off -f) chooses, for
    optimisation, the value of a variable with up to 64 values by trying
    each one and taking the one that leaves the best bound on the
    objective, as local search chooses its moves; ties keep the value of
    the last solution. The probes take at most a quarter of the time, so
    proofs of optimality are slowed by at most that much. --free-config
    value-impact=<n> (0 = off) and value-impact-time=<share>. Against the
    adaptive LNS of #322 on 119 instances (60 s): primal integral 21%
    lower, 21 better and 16 worse final objectives (gbac UD5 280 against
    6585; Yuck reaches 977, CP-SAT 372). Measured against Yuck and
    Atlantis in osolpro benchmarks/lns-ls.

92. Presolve rules have timing classes, as in SCIP: fast (the
    propagation-like rules), medium (at-most-one, redundant constraints,
    cumulative reductions) and exhaustive (probing, tabling, implied
    constraints). A round runs the costlier class only after a round of
    the cheaper ones changed fewer than --presolve-abort (default 0.001)
    times the variables, and the rounds stop after a small round at the
    top class or when the budget is spent (--presolve-time, default 1/20
    of the time left). --presolve-level fast|medium|exhaustive caps the
    class. When the rules simplified nothing, the store is searched in
    place instead of being copied: the copy alone changed the search
    (cars_60_04 went unsolved). Under --lcg, rule 8 no longer substitutes
    doubletons, which lengthened explanations (diameterc-mst 5 to 24
    atoms); -Djacop.presolve.doubletons=on brings it back. The time of
    every phase is printed as presolveTime<Phase> and the rounds per
    class as presolveRounds<Class>. --presolve-restart <f> (off by
    default) marks the constraints satisfied at the root entailed at a
    restart once the root fixed f times the variables more. On the 249
    mznbench instances, --lcg, 20 s: fast presolve with --symmetry off
    solves the same 134 as presolve off with 18 better objectives and 3
    worse (Borda 223.6 against 214.6; full presolve 206.8), at a median
    0.06 s more set-up; with -f it still loses (157 against 159 solved,
    Borda 335.0 against 355.0), and --presolve-restart 0.025 is about
    neutral there (158 solved, Borda 363.4). Presolve stays off by
    default.

93. New constraint Cumulatives (org.jacop.constraints.cumulative), the
    cumulatives of Beldiceanu and Carlsson: tasks with variable starts,
    durations, heights and machines, a bound per machine that is an
    upper or a lower limit on the load wherever a task runs, heights
    that may be negative, and optional tasks. A sweep over each
    machine's least-load profile prunes starts, machines, durations,
    heights, presence and bounds; it also forces a negative task to run
    where the others alone break the bound, a deduction the forbidden
    regions miss. Under --lcg it explains its pruning and failures.
    MiniZinc cumulatives with lower bounds or negative heights, and the
    optional form, use it instead of the quadratic standard library
    decomposition (upper bounds with non-negative heights still go to
    one cumulative per machine). No Challenge model uses cumulatives; on
    29 generated coverage, paired-task and power instances, 60 s:
    --lcg off 12 better, none worse (cover_10_3_0 optimum in 4.8 s
    instead of 42 s, three power instances find a solution); --lcg
    7 better and 7 worse (pairs_24_5_2 UNSAT not proved, power proofs
    up to 2x slower, cover_24_5_1 a first solution).

94. The experimental cut-based conflict analysis of --lcg
    (-Djacop.lcg.linear=learn, still off by default) also combines
    linear rows of non-linear constraints, made once on the root bounds:
    big-M rows of reified and implied linear relations (and of FlatZinc
    x = y and x = c implied by a Boolean), and/or, max/min, |x| and the
    McCormick rows of x*y. It also starts from an LP failure and uses the
    LP's bound prunings as reasons, through the new LinearReasons
    interface of LinearRelaxation. A failing constraint that is itself
    asserting is no longer learnt again as a copy. On the 249 mznbench
    instances, 10 s, failures that end in a learnt sum after at least one
    step went from 2.5% to 8.0% (--lcg -f) and from 2.1% to 7.0%
    (--lcg). 30 s with 30 terms at most: on the 102 instance/mode pairs
    that learn, 2 reps, --lcg -f 18 better and 9 worse, --lcg 15 and 2
    (still_life_8x8 proved in 10 s, hrc_exp2-1-4795 and maximum-dag
    faster, bus_scheduling r4 and t2 faster); over the whole set, --lcg
    13 and 11, --lcg -f 21 and 17, with 11 and 2 (11 and 3 under -f)
    where the analysis learns.

95. Geost checks volumes: in every dimension of a non-overlapping group
    that selects all spatial dimensions (and time, if selected), the
    objects that must lie inside a window must fit there by volume,
    within the hull of those objects and the InArea box. It is on by
    default; Geost.setVolumeReasoning(false) turns it off. New external
    constraint LexChain(objects) orders the origins of interchangeable
    objects lexicographically inside the sweep (symmetry breaking, like
    SICStus geost lex); it is opt-in. Shape.area() returned 0 for
    shapes made of one box; it is fixed. On the Geost benchmarks (60 s,
    input order search) volume takes squares13 from 23,031 to 4,083
    nodes and proves jobs12 and pallet2x5-12x9-11 infeasible at the
    root (101,583 nodes and the time limit before); LexChain proves
    pallet3x5-13x13-11 infeasible in 4.6 s, which times out without it.
    No MiniZinc Challenge instance 2008-2026 reaches Geost.

96. With --lcg and an objective, free search (-f) gives way to free search
    without learning when LCG is too slow: if, at a tenth of the time
    limit or later, LCG has made fewer than 50 conflicts per second and
    has a solution, it stops, the model's clauses are posted to the store
    and free search continues from the LCG solution (phase hints) with
    its cost as the bound. --free-config lcg-slow-rate=<conflicts per
    second> (0 turns it off) and lcg-slow-at=<share of the time limit>
    set the rule; the statistic lcgSlowConflicts reports a switch. On the
    59 Challenge instances that -f has not finished at 6 s (60 s), 12
    switch: 7 end better than both runs without the rule (tdtsp 20_26
    19,830 to 12,995, cvrp B-n45-k5 2,084 to 1,587), 3 worse (cargo 08
    and 16, jobshop swv07) and 2 equal. ptv 1c goes from 2,941 to 1,666,
    ptv 5a from 270 to 60, cgt_12 from 1 to -129,352 and cgt_3 from
    19,722 to -147,892.
    
97. Portfolio (-p) settings are chosen from data. Each setting was run
    alone on 240 hard instances (osolpro benchmarks/portfolio-configs) and
    the threads after the first now follow the greedy best order for both
    -f (--lcg on -f) and no options (--lcg on): LCG with literal branching,
    free search with CHS and Luby restarts, the annotation without LCG,
    the annotation with LCG, then the rest. A setting that would run the
    same search as an earlier thread is skipped: objective-only options
    (lcg-core, lcg-lb, lns, probe) on a satisfaction model and share-*
    options do not count, so lcg-bb no longer repeats lcg on satisfaction
    models. The LCG core-guided phase stops when its lower bound meets the
    bound shared by the portfolio, and hardens with it; before, it used
    only its own best solution. -f -p 4, 60 s, 169 hard instances, two
    rounds that run both builds on each instance in turn: 99 solved
    instead of 95 and 101 instead of 92, 61 and 59 instances better
    against 25 and 26 worse, proofs about 26% faster. Routing and large
    scheduling models reach somewhat worse objectives, since plain -f
    without LCG is no longer among the first four threads.

98. fzn-jacop -s prints only the standard FlatZinc statistics (nodes,
    failures, restarts, peakDepth, variables, propagators, propagations,
    nogoods, initTime, solveTime, objective, solutions) and now peakMem,
    the peak memory of the JVM in Mbytes. The statistics of JaCoP's own
    components (lcg*, presolve*, lp*, lns*, ls*, symmetry*, probe*,
    portfolio*, objectiveProbes*, goalSubproblems, nodesPerSecond and the
    per-class "%% lcg" lines) need the new option --search-statistics
    (which implies -s), or -s together with -debug. The MiniZinc solver
    configuration declares --search-statistics. A malformed
    "%%%%mzn-stat: restarts" line of -v output is fixed.

99. Under --lcg, the FlatZinc statistics report peakDepth, the deepest
    decision level of the SAT search (assumption levels included), and
    restarts, the SAT solver's restarts (as lcgRestarts). Before, both
    were always 0.

100. The FlatZinc front end solves the independent parts of a model that
    the objective does not depend on (for a satisfaction model, every
    part but the largest) once at the root, and fixes them. Each part
    gets a depth-first search in annotation order with a 2 s budget
    (-Djacop.components.budget, in ms); a part without a solution makes
    the model infeasible, and a part that runs out of time is left to
    the main search. It is skipped with -a, with -n > 1 for satisfaction
    and with on_restart. --components off turns it off. Constraints
    that watch only some of their variables (long Boolean sums, clauses)
    link all their variables. 92 of the 1,781 MiniZinc Challenge
    instances split into several parts; on the 109 checked, 60 s: same
    statuses, ghoulomb 3-7-20 and 3-8-20 prove their optimum in 2 s
    instead of 15 s, 3-10-20 in 15 s instead of 38 s, 2013 4-9-20 in
    4 s instead of 17 s, and cable_tree_wiring R046 ends at its optimum
    143,534 instead of 284,142; other objectives move by noise.

101. Presolve dual reductions also find dominated columns: if raising x
    by one and lowering y by one keeps every constraint and the
    objective, the clause x = max(x) or y = min(y) is added (x >= y for
    0-1 variables). It is left out with -a, since it can remove some of
    the improving solutions printed on the way to the optimum (the
    optimum stays). -Djacop.presolve.domcol=false turns it off. It fires
    on 37 Challenge instances (train, table-layout, connect,
    unit-commitment, wmsmc-int) and is neutral on them at 60 s.

102. fzn-jacop --proof <prefix> writes a VeriPB 3 certificate of an LCG
    search: <prefix>.opb is the model as pseudo-Boolean constraints over
    the order encoding of the domains the FlatZinc translation gives the
    variables, before the constraints propagate, and <prefix>.pbp the proof (rup steps for learnt clauses and lazy
    reasons, pol steps for the flow reasons of cardinality constraints,
    soli for improving solutions, deletions, and a conclusion UNSAT, SAT
    or BOUNDS lb ub) for veripb <prefix>.opb <prefix>.pbp.
    It is off by default, implies --lcg, one thread, bounds consistency
    and no LP, and runs no core-guided phase and learns no linear sums.
    Linear constraints (LinearInt, LinearBool and the linear forms of LCG
    explanations), their reifications, equalities with constants,
    ChannelReif, ChannelImply, clauses, element over constants, Count,
    AtMost, AtLeast, CountBounds, Max, Min, tables, regular and MDD
    constraints, Alldifferent, Alldiff, Alldistinct, GCC, CountValues and
    CountValuesBounds are encoded (the flow reasons of the last six by a
    pol step over a violated cut of their flow network), as are
    b -> x + c <= z and the time table of CumulativeBasic, with fixed or
    variable durations and resource uses (a time-indexed encoding); under
    --proof every cumulative is posted as CumulativeBasic, and the
    redundant constraints the front end adds (unary resources, energy
    precedence, weighted completion, stocking cost, overlapping all
    different) are left out; so are diffn (as Nooverlap without its area
    and count failures, every pair of rectangles a clause of the four
    ways they are apart over order literals), int_times (as a table of
    the products) and element over variables (ElementVariableFast);
    for another constraint, or a
    model over 4*10^8 characters, fzn-jacop prints "%% --proof: ..." and
    =====UNKNOWN=====. The proof derives each change of the root
    propagation as a unit from its reason, and a failure of the root
    propagation, or a clause false at the root, as UNSAT; changes without a reason (domains narrowed by
    the translation) or by a constraint that cannot be encoded narrow the
    model's domains instead. The FlatZinc translation is not certified.

103. FlatZinc regular with a set alphabet (jacop_regular_set) read the
    next states of its transition table shifted by the smallest symbol
    instead of by 1. With an alphabet such as 0..1 it crashed with an
    ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException; with one from 2 it could map
    transitions to the wrong state and report UNSAT for satisfiable
    models. Alphabets from 1 (the usual case) were not affected.

104. LCG explanations of GCC, GlobalCardinality and CountValues first try
     counting reasons: a counter's upper bound from the variables that
     exclude its value or from the lower bounds of the other counters, a
     counter's lower bound from the variables fixed to its value, and a
     value removed (or a bound moved past values) because other variables
     fixed to it reach its counter's bound. Only when none applies is the
     flow network built, which has a time share and gave the generic
     reason beyond it (93 % of GCC reasons on fillomino 7x7_0).
     -Djacop.lcg.cardinalityCounts=false turns them off. ArgMax and ArgMin
     reasons skip values outside the initial domain, where a bound across
     a wide hole gave the generic reason (47 % on tower). On the wide
     benchmark set (220 instances, 60 s) --lcg is 10 better / 6 worse and
     --lcg -f solves 140 instead of 136; on the 17 instances with these
     constraints 22 better / 6 worse over 5 runs (fillomino 7x7_0 solved,
     league 15.4 s to 8.8 s).

105. fzn-jacop breaks symmetries by default, also without --presolve:
    lex-leader constraints for variable symmetries, lex chains for
    interchangeable columns and value precedence for interchangeable
    values, as --presolve on did (--symmetry off turns it off). It is
    left out for all solutions of satisfaction problems and for models
    loaded with FlatzincLoader. When the search annotation tries the
    largest values first (indomain_max, indomain_reverse_split), the
    lex-leader keeps the largest solution of each orbit, so the search
    meets it first. Free search (-f) leaves value precedence out of
    models that minimize the largest load of a bin packing (P||Cmax),
    where it multiplied the failures of --lcg -f (pcmax n41-m7-s18:
    30,015 to 214; 20 instances, 30 s: 16 to 19 proved). The second
    given to symmetry detection now also
    bounds reading the model and building its graphs, which took up to
    7.5 s on groupsplitter and ma-path-finding. The FlatZinc test suites
    run with -Djacop.symmetry=presolve, which keeps the old behaviour.
    Symmetry constraints are posted on 431 of the 1,781 MiniZinc
    Challenge instances (2008-2025); detection takes at most 1 s,
    median 0.1 s. On the 376 of them not solved in 2 s, 30 s: fixed
    search solves 141 -> 145, -f 189 -> 194; on 140 generated colouring,
    bin packing and scheduling models 81 -> 91 and 87 -> 95.

105. LCG explanations of set constraints. What a set's domain derives
     after a set operation changes it (a cardinality bound, an element
     forced in or out by a cardinality bound, a cardinality failure) is
     explained by the operation from the elements its relation forces,
     instead of the generic reason. set_card (CardAeqX) explains by the
     set's cardinality rules with the cardinality variable, and
     array_set_element (ElementSet) by the elements or the cardinality
     that exclude an index and by the indexes left. The generic share of
     these constraints on monomatch, vaccine, gt-sort and valve-network
     falls to 0 (valve-network: 99.8 % of ElementSet reasons). On the 8
     wide-set instances with set constraints (60 s, 3 seeds per mode)
     --lcg is 6 better / 2 worse (monomatch n_5 solved in 4 s, which
     timed out; gt-sort 131 to 127) and --lcg -f 7 / 6.

Version 4.11
=============

1. Changes in XexpYeqZ to be compatible with minizinc definition for
   negative exponents. Domain consistency implemented.

2. Falzinc definitions for asinh, acosh and atanh.

3. New constraints AlldifferentExceptZero and AlldifferentExcept
   available also from minizinc.

4. New constraints ElementBool available also from minizinc.

5. Definition of impact variable selection method for minizinc.

6. Bug corrections, code cleaning and performance improvements.

Version 4.10
=============

1. Implementation of relax and reconstruct for restart
   search. Available in minizinc with search annotation
   relax_and_reconstruct in combination with restart annotation.

2. Implementation of round, floor and ceil constraints in both JaCoP
   (RoundPeqX, FloorPeqX and CeilPeqX) and for minizinc.

3. Implementation of channeling constraint, ChannelIntSet and
   ChannelBoolSet. ChannelIntSet is defined for array of int variables
   x and array of set variables s. They are related such that (x[i] =
   j) <-> (i in y[j]). ChannelBoolSet id defined for array of Boolean
   variables and a set variable and requires that i in s <-> b[i].
   Available in minizinc as int_set_channel and Link_set_to_booleans.

4. Implementation of set partitioning constraint, PartitionSet, and
   related minizinc interface.

5. Implementation of cumulative constraints, CumulativeOptional and
   CumulativeUnaryOptional, for optional tasks. Available from
   minizinc as cumulative and disjunctive with start times defined as
   optional variables.

6. Implementation of AllEqual primitive constraint (can be reified)
   and SeqPrecedeChain constraint (not incremental version since it
   requires many TimeStamp variables and informing constraint which
   variables have changed). Both constraints are available from
   minizinc.

7. Command line options in flatzinc for restart search.

8. Bug corrections, code cleaning and performance improvements.

Version 4.9
=============

1. New constraints, Increasing and Decreasing, both for strict (<) and
   non-strict (=<) ordering.

2. Adding to minizinc graph constraints on (sub-)graph isomorphism,
   matching and clique. Prepared to use package org.jacop.graph that
   will make it possible to used graph global constraints
   instead. This is controlled by variable use_jacop_graph_constraints
   = true in minizinc library file jacop.mzn.

3. Random number generators can start from a given seed defined by
   Store method setSeed(long seed). Faltzinc accepts option -r
   (--random-seed) that defines the seed.

4. Support for a new variable selection annotation for search (in
   minizinc).  Annotation "tiebreak" takes two parameters (two
   variable selection methods) and uses the first one as the main
   selection method and the second one as tie breaking, in case when
   both main selection methods has the same value. Example
   tiebreak([first_fail,occurrence]).

5. Support for standard "warm_start" search annotation for int and
   bool variables in minizinc. It starts search with a specified value
   for a given variable and then continues with either indomain_min or
   indomain_max.

6. New constraint generation for table constraints in flatzinc
   interpreter. In cases when up to three variables are defined and
   there exist up to three conflicting tuples JaCoP Or constraints are
   generated instead.

7. Bug corrections, code cleaning and performance improvements.

Version 4.8
=============

1. Removing -f (format) and -p (precision) option flags. Flatzinc uses
   instead --format and --precision. Option -f is used to denote free
   search, based on AFCMaxDeg/AFCMax variable selection method. The
   default search (no search annotations) still uses the old search
   method for compatibility reasons.

2. Improving debug information print-out for flatzinc.

3. Improvement in propagation for XinA constraint.

4. Improvement of propagation for ArgMin and ArgMax.

5. Generation of IfThenElse constraint instead of Conditional for
   flatzinc if_then_else_* constraints in cases when it is possible.

6. Improvement in pruning of task duration in Cumulative Basic
   constraint.

7. New implementation of counting constraints in flatzinc. Some
   compile to Atleast and AtMost. Original at_least and at_most are
   deprecated from version 2.4 of Minizinc.

8. New CountVar constraint and its flatzinc interface.

9. New global cardinality constraint CountValues and
   CountValuesBounds. Changes in minizinc library for global
   cardinality constraints to use these two new constraints.

10. New constraint Channel that generalizes Reified
    constraint. Available in Java and minizinc. Several reified
    constraints on the same variable are converted to a single channel
    constraint in flatzinc.

11. Removing limitation in flatzinc compiler that flatzinc variables
    must have domains within interval
    IntDomain.MinInt..IntDomain.MaxInt. First constraints might report
    arithmetic overflow.

12. Changing Scala compiler to 2.13.1 and making related code changes.

13. Not recognized search options in flatzinc are ignored instead of
    aborting computation.

14. Changing IntDomain.divIntBounds method to use double instead of floats
    for bounds calculations (better precision).

15. Constraints XplusYplusQeqZ, XplusYplusCeqZ and XplusYplusQeqC are
    removed. Use SumInt instead.

16. Additional CountBounds constraint added in flatzinc translation of
    global_cardinality_low_up_closed if domain consistency annotation
    is present (GCC does bounds consistency only).

17. Bug corrections and performance improvements.

Version 4.7
=============

1. Implementation of restart search both in JaCoP and flatzinc (uses
   standard minizinc annotation).

2. Implementation of priority search both in JaCoP and in flatzinc
   (uses annotation priority_search; check JaCoP specific library
   definition at jacop.mzn for the definition).

3. Making WeightedDegree variable selection method working correctly;
   available in flatzinc as dom_w_deg. Adding new variable selection
   methods based on accumulated failure count (AFC) and pruning
   activity; defined in flatzinc also.

4. Time-out in flatzinc is changed to be specified in milliseconds (to
   be compatible with minizinc options).

5. New distance step option for search with cost function specified as
   FloatVar. Available in both Java and flatzinc.

6. Small corrections in pom.xml to use right version of Scala.

7. Restructuring of minizinc library with the new method introduced in
   Minizinc 2.3. It uses fzn_*.mzn files to define JaCoP specific
   global constraint

8. Definitions in redefintions.mzn and implementation of half-reified
   constraints, that is minizinc constraints with the name *_imp. Implementation
   of JaCoP constraint Implies.

9. Definitions and implementation of fzn_if_then_else_* constraints in
   flatzinc JaCoP library and in Java (constraint Conditional).

10. Implementation of Member constraint and making it available from
    flatzinc (added into the JaCoP library).

11. Flatzinc annotation "priority(k)" defining constraint priority
    (0..4) where 0 is the highest and 4 the lowest priority of a constraint.

12. New format for methods toString() for depth first search
    methods. Flatzinc compiler prints search in debug mode.

13. Bug corrections.

Version 4.6
=============

1. Replacing buggy Lex constraint for sets with simple but correct
   AltB and AleB. Both constraints implement lexical order between
   sets using either "<" or "<=" relations. Lex is kept for
   compatibility.
   Constraints AltB and AleB are primitive and can be used as
   parameters to other constraints. Implementation of flatzinc
   set_lt_reif and set_le_reif.

2. SumInt, SumBool, LinearInt, LinearIntDom and LinearFloat does not
   need Store parameter any longer.

3. Fix of null pointer exception in Binpacking constraint. It is
   sometimes present if a bin variable is ground when constraint is
   imposed. Improving performance of Binpacking constraint by running
   computationally expensive lower bound computations only when
   constraint is fix-point.

4. New constraint for symmetry breaking, ValuePrecede.

5. Implementation of ElementFloatVariable and ElementSetVariable as
   well as related flatzinc predicates array_var_float_element
   array_var_set_element.

6. New version of Alldiff constraints that uses new algorithm with
   complexity O(n logn) instead of O(n^2).

7. Improvements in subcircuit constraint. It applies computationally
   expensive dominance pruning if SCC did not pruned anything.

8. New primitive constraints AtLeast and AtMost. Adding generation of
   these constraints and their reified versions for flatzinc.

9. Adding print-out of intermediate statistics for search (search nodes
   and execution time) to flatzinc when option -v is given.

10. Adding JaCoP solver configuration file, 'org.jacop.msc', for
    minizinc 2.2 (both stand-alone and IDE) and fzn-jacop script to be
    used to call JaCoP solve.

11. New print-out of statistics for flatzinc that follows minizinc 2.2
    standard.

12. Definition of flatzinc library global constraint
    alldifferent_except0 using global_cardinality_low_up_closed.
    Related changes of generation of JaCoP GCC constraint.

13. Bug fix in sequential search when there is float_search after
    set_search.

Version 4.5
=============

1. Performance improvements and identification of the same items in
   Binpacking constraint.

2. Correcting definitions of bin_packing and bin_packing_capa in JaCoP
    flatzinc library.

3. New constructor for LinearInt and LInearIntDom that makes it easier
   to define it. Makes it possible to specify result as IntVar.

4. New constraint SumFloat and new constructor for LinearFloat. Makes
   it possible to specify result as FloatVar.

5. New JaCoP specific variable selection options in flatzinc
   (smallest_max, smallest_most_constrained, smallest_first_fail). It
   requires to include jacop.mzn in minizinc program.

6. Adding soft_all_different_int and soft_global_cardinality_int to
   flatzinc. It requires to include jacop.mzn in minizinc program.

7. Printing BooleanVar when store is printed.

8. Adding possibility to Scala wrapper to define element constraint
   on a list of BoolVar as array access.

9. Adding floating-point constraints log2 and log10 to flatzinc to
   JaCoP compiler.

10. Two new classes for debugging and statistics gathering.
   VariableTrace prints out variables prunings during search.
   FailConstraintsStatistics gather number of fails for each
   constraints and class of constraints.

11. New cumulative constraints. CumulativeBasic does time-tabling
    propagation, Cumulative does time-tabling and edge finding
    propagation, and CumulativeUnary is a specialized version of unary
    resources and does in addition to two previous propagations
    not-first-not-last, detectable and overload propagations. All
    propagations, except time-tabling, are done on binary trees and
    have the following complexities, where n is the number of tasks
    and k is the number of distinct resource values for tasks. Edge
    finding O(k*n*logn), not-first-not-last, detectable and overload
    O(n*logn).

12. New, more efficient implementation of Diffn constraint. Added
    Nooveralp constraint implementing basic non-overlapping
    conditions for rectangles and DiffnDecomposed implemented as three
    constraints: Nooverlap and two CumulativeBasic constraints in
    direction X and Y.

13. New Table and SimpleTable (number of table entries =< 64)
    constraints based on compact-table representation.

14. Calls to new cumulative, diffn and table constraints in Scala wrapper.

15. New method for constraint generation for flatzinc.

16. Making JaCoP compatible with Scala 2.12.

17. Improvement in pruning for MinSimple and MaxSimple.

18. Bug fixes:
   - Changing comparator method in LinearFloat that
     IllegalArgumentException is not reported.
   - Correcting bugs in AndBool, OrBool and AndBoolSimple.
   - Correcting a bug when a constraint needs information of changed
     variables and is a parameter to other constraint, for example
     Reified, Xor.
   - Bug fix when reified BoolClause is generated in falzinc. The case
     when BoolClause fails and reified variable is false.
   - Bug fix in SumInt that stopped the propagator from computing a
     fix-point in some situations.
   - Bug fix in method inComplement that did not generated event
     "BOUND" when bounds in the domain was changed (generated "ANY"
     instead).

19. Different Maven profiles for testing (commit, push, release) that
    will execute different lengths tests in Maven test phase using
    Minizinc tests stored in src/test/fz/ directory.

20. Various code cleaning based on complaints from findbugs, Intelij IDE,
    and Sonar.

21. Adding workaround for a bug in Geost caused by too eager ignoring
    execution of propagation when constraint is not consistent.

22. Extending testing suites by adding thousands of minizinc based tests,
    as well as some unit tests for domain classes. Adding fznFileGenerator
    skrypt to quickly categorize new minizinc problems into time categories
    to be used by future tests.

23. Cleaning up design around Constraints. Refactoring a lot of code from
    particular constraints into common functionality in Constraint.
	
Version 4.4
===========

1. New selection for search variables in minizinc. Only output
   variables are selected by default.

2. Adding additional pass to the flatzinc compiler. bool2int does not
   generate the equality constraints on both variables. Only the first
   variable is used in the model and the second one as the alias for
   the first one. Decreases the number of generated constraints.

3. Sum and SumWeight are deprecated and SumInt, SumBool or LinearInt
   should be used instead.

4. New circuit and subcircuit constraint definitions in minizinc that
   accept arbitrary indexes (not always starting from 1).

5. New constraint SumBool.

6. Correcting a bug in OrBool constraint.

7. Xor of Linear constraint corrected. Linear is deprecated and
   LinearInt should be used instead.

8. Correcting a bug in GCC.

9. Improvements and corrections in Linear, LinearInt, SumInt,
   LinearIntDom and LinearFloat.

10. Bug fixes and improvements.

Version 4.3
===========
1. Implementation of constraints ArgMin and ArgMax as well connection to
   flatzinc constraints arg_min_int and arg_max_int.

2. Correcting implementation of Diff2 and Disjoint. Currently Diff2
   implements non-strict version of diffn constraint (minizinc constraint
   diffn_nonstrict) that makes it possible to pack zero-width rectangles
   anywhere. Disjoint implements a strict version of diffn constraint
   (minizinc diffn constraint). It requires that zero-width rectangles
   does not overlap with any other rectangle.
	
3. Implementation of primitive constraint BoolClause implementing flatzinc
   constraint bool_clause.
	
4. Sets of integer with bounds 1..0 are treated by flatzinc parser as
   empty sets. This implements a way minizinc 2.0 represents empty sets.

5. Adding int_pow build-in predicate to flatzinc interpreter.

6. LexOrder global constraints accepts vectors of different sizes.

7. Performance improvement for SumWeightDom.

8. Performance improvement for ElementInteger and two new constraints
   ElementIntegerFast and ElementVariableFast that implement a
   straightforward pruning method.

9. New primitive constraints LinearInt and SumInt that implement
   pruning methods from paper "Bounds Consistency Techniques for Long
   Linear Constraints" by Warwick Harvey and Joachim Schimpf.

10. Fixing performance bug in IntervalBasedBacktrackableManager.
    Unnecessary call to checkRemoveInvariant outside assert statement.

11. Adding option --bound (-b) to flatzinc parser that enforces use of bounds
    consistency for constraints (instead of domain, even for constraints
    annotated with "::domain").

12. Adding SAT solver to JaCoP and connecting it to flatzinc
    interpreter. The use of SAT solver in flatzinc is controlled by
    option -sat. If the option is specified the SAT solver is used for
    all bool_* constraints, otherwise CP constraints are used.

13. Improving performance of Max and Min constraints. Specially for
    large lists of varibales.

14. Bug fixes.
	
Version 4.2
===========
1. Support for minizinc 2.0
2. Constraint Geost avaible is now from minizinc.
3. Implementation of Solution-Guided Multi-Point Constructive Search.
4. Improvements in minimization method for floating-point variables.

Version 4.1.1
===========

1. New global constraint LexOrder that defines lexicographical order
   between two vectors of IntVar. Vectors must be of the same size.
2. Implementation of a loader for flazinc models and related examples. The
   loader makes it possible to load the model and then define own search
   methods as well as use all variables defined in the model and their values.
3. Performance improvements in AndBool and OrBool. it is no longer needed
   to scan the whole list for achieving consistency.
4. Checking for overflow in Sum and improvement in detecting overflow for
   addition.
5. Since domain consistency is computationally expensive minizinc uses
   domain consistency for linear constraints only when constrained are
   annotated ":: domain" and the size of maximal domains is <= 4 and
   there is no more than 2 variables.

Version 4.1
===========

1. New floating point variables and constraints on these variables.

2. Flatzinc interface for JaCoP's floating point constraints.

3. Scala interface for floating point constraints.

4. Fixing a bug in knapsack constraints when large numbers are used.

5. Implementing intersection method between SmallDenseDomain and BoundDomain.

Version 4.0
===========

1. Maven support and new directory structure. The package has changed name from JaCoP to org.jacop and files are placed in the new directory structure.

2. Experimental feature - stochastic variables and constraints - is moved to feature branch as it still requires additional testing.
   It is excluded from this release, but we encourage contribution in this branch (examples/tests in particular) to help us make it
   quicker a part of the next release.

3. Experimental feature SAT solver - JaSAT - is moved to experimental feature branch as its performance and integration with JaCoP still
   requires extra work. It is excluded from this release, but we encourage contribution in this branch to help us make it quicker a part
    of the next release.

4. Adding constraint SumWeightDom that implements domain consistency
   for weighted sum of linear terms.

5. New primitive constraint Linear and its use in flatzinc.

6. New global constraint Subcircuit.

7. Adding additional propagation to Min and Max constraint. It
   propagates in situations when one variable on the list is lowest
   (greatest). It enforces that this variable from the list is equal
   min (max) variable.

8. Print of statistics for flatzinc (org.jacop.fz.Fz2jacop) after Ctrl-C.

9. Updated JaCoP guide to reflect new features.

10. Bug fixes.

